    "key_characteristics": "HBV and HIV co-infection transmitted perinatally; HBV resistance to lamivudine after prolonged intermittent treatment; clinical resistance to tenofovir manifesting as HBV-DNA breakthrough; non-compliance reasonably excluded; HIV-RNA constantly suppressed."
  },
  "intervention": {
    "name": "Entecavir added to failing therapy with tenofovir and emtricitabine",
    "dose": "1 mg",
    "frequency": "daily",
    "duration": "over 12 months"
  },
  "outcomes": [
    {
      "measure": "HBV-DNA suppression",
      "timepoint": "after adding entecavir, over 12 months",
      "effect": "rapid and continuous suppression",
      "ci": null
    },
    {
      "measure": "HIV-RNA suppression",
      "timepoint": "during tenofovir resistance episode",
      "effect": "constantly suppressed",
      "ci": null
    },
    {
      "measure": "virological success",
      "timepoint": "after adding entecavir to tenofovir and emtricitabine",
      "effect": "achieved",
      "ci": null
    }
  ],
  "safety": [
    {
      "event": "adverse events",
      "rate": "none reported; treatment was well-tolerated and safe"
    }
  ],
  "other_facts": "Nucleos(t)ide analogs such as tenofovir, lamivudine, or emtricitabine are active against both HBV and HIV; tenofovir confers potent and durable HBV-DNA suppression, but the best strategy for HBV resistance to tenofovir remains unknown; after prolonged and intermittent treatment of HIV with lamivudine and tenofovir, HBV became resistant to lamivudine; clinical resistance to tenofovir occurred manifesting as HBV-DNA breakthrough; adding entecavir to failing therapy with tenofovir and emtricitabine was feasible; no known mutations such as rtA181T/V associated with rtN236T or A194T associated with reduced susceptibility or resistance to tenofovir were detected; a unique and complex HBV substitution pattern was found with development of rtR192PR at the time of virological failure; rtR192PR is located in the B domain near rtA194T, occurring in a context of very complex substitution patterns, and might be associated with resistance to tenofovir.",
  "summary": "In a 22-year-old perinatally HBV/HIV-coinfected patient with tenofovir resistance and a complex HBV polymerase substitution pattern including rtR192PR, adding entecavir 1 mg daily to tenofovir and emtricitabine was feasible, well tolerated, and produced sustained HBV-DNA suppression for over 12 months."
}

