  "other_facts": "Circulating polyunsaturated fatty acids have been implicated in neurodegenerative processes, but epidemiological evidence remains inconsistent. Genetic susceptibility to Alzheimer's disease, commonly indexed by a polygenic risk score, is a major determinant of dementia risk. Few large-scale prospective studies have jointly examined plasma fatty acid profiles and genetic liability to Alzheimer's disease in relation to incident dementia. The objectives were to investigate the independent and combined associations of plasma omega-3 PUFAs, omega-6 PUFAs, and the omega-6/omega-3 ratio with incident dementia and to determine whether these associations vary according to AD-PRS. Plasma fatty acids were quantified using standardized nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy. Genetic risk for Alzheimer's disease was assessed using the Enhanced AD-PRS. Incident dementia was identified through linked hospital admission and death registry records. Multivariable-adjusted hazard ratios were estimated using Cox proportional hazards models. Dose-response relationships and both additive and multiplicative interactions with AD-PRS were evaluated, alongside subgroup and sensitivity analyses. Over a median follow-up of 12.1 years, 1,335 participants developed dementia. P values were P < 0.001 for omega-6, P = 0.81 for omega-3, and P = 0.55 for the omega-6/omega-3 ratio. Findings were robust across multiple sensitivity analyses. Higher plasma omega-6 PUFA levels were independently associated with a clinically meaningful reduction in dementia risk, whereas omega-3 PUFAs and the omega-6/omega-3 ratio were not. Elevated omega-6 modestly reduced absolute risk among individuals with high genetic susceptibility to Alzheimer's disease, but no meaningful gene-nutrient interaction was detected. Further mechanistic and interventional studies are warranted to elucidate underlying pathways.",
  "summary": "In a prospective UK Biobank cohort of 81,827 participants followed for a median of 12.1 years, higher plasma omega-6 PUFA levels were associated with lower incident dementia risk, while omega-3 levels and the omega-6/omega-3 ratio were not, and no meaningful interaction with Alzheimer's disease genetic risk was detected."
}

