  "other_facts": "The aim was to determine the effect of Nigella sativa supplementation on liver and kidney parameters. PubMed, Scopus, ISI Web of Science, Cochrane central register for controlled trials, and Google Scholar were searched from database inception to April 2019. Mean differences and standard deviations for each outcome were pooled using a random-effects model. Dose-response analysis was performed using a fractional polynomial model. Quality of evidence was evaluated using the Cochrane Collaboration Risk of Bias tool and GRADE methodology. N.S supplements had significant reducing effects on ALP and BUN concentrations. Subgroup analysis found that an intervention of more than 12 weeks reduced AST measures. Creatinine levels increased significantly in studies that considered adjusted analysis based on covariates. A daily dose of 1100-1500 mg of N.S supplements had a substantial reducing effect on ALP levels. A dosage of more than 2000 mg per day was reported to lead to a significant increase in BUN concentrations. The authors concluded that N.S supplementation had significant impacts on liver and kidney parameters, with longer intervention duration and normal daily dosages leading to reductions in ALP and AST, respectively, while higher daily dosages increased BUN levels; they stated that more high-quality, large-scale, long-term intervention studies with precise baseline characteristics are needed to assess the exact effective dose, duration, and efficacy of N.S supplementation on kidney and liver parameters.",
