  "outcomes": null,
  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Neurospora crassa is the model filamentous fungus for the study of many fundamental cellular mechanisms of transport and metabolism. The recently completed genome sequence of N. crassa has over 10,000 genes, and 41% of these genes have no significant matches in sequence databases, presenting many challenges for new discoveries. Using a transporter database and BLAST searches, a total of 65 open reading frames for putative cation transporter genes were identified in N. crassa. These were confirmed by characteristic family features including transmembrane domains using TOPPRED 2, conserved motifs using Clustal W, and phylogenetic analysis using TREETOP. In Neurospora, cation transporter genes constitute nearly 18.3% of the total membrane transport systems, which is higher than E. coli (8.8%), S. cerevisiae (13.7%), S. pombe (17.2%), A. fumigatus (10.1%), A. thaliana (16.8%), and H. sapiens (15.6%). The complete complement of metal ion transporter genes is referred to as the Metal Transportome. There are 33 putative transporters for alkali and alkaline earth metals: 18 for calcium (P-ATPase, VIC, CaCA, Mid1), 7 for sodium (P-ATPase, CPA1, CPA2), 4 for potassium (Trk, VIC, KUP), and 4 for magnesium (MIT). Transition metal ion transporters account for 32 transporters, including 7 for zinc (ZIP), 6 for copper (Ctr2, Ctr1), 2 each for manganese (Nramp), iron (OFeT), arsenite (ArsAB, ACR3), and other metal ions (ABC and P-ATPase), and 1 each for nickel (NiCoT) and chromate (CHR). N. crassa has 7 linkage groups; linkage group I harbors 21 metal ion transporters, whereas linkage group VII has only 2. Studies on metal transportomes of different organisms will help to unravel the role of metal ion transporters in homeostasis.",
  "summary": "This bioinformatic genomic analysis identified and characterized 65 putative cation transporter genes in Neurospora crassa, defined its metal transportome, and compared the proportion of cation transporters with those of other organisms."
}

