  "other_facts": "Immune checkpoint inhibitors have been extensively used and significantly improved clinical outcomes in multiple types of cancer; immune-related adverse events occur frequently, particularly in thymoma; cardiac immune-related adverse events are relatively rare but fatal and have been increasingly reported. The patient received anti-programmed cell death-1 treatment with sintilimab 14 days before admission. Additional interventions included intravenous immunoglobulin 25 g/day; methylprednisolone reduced to 40 mg/day for the next 3 days; intravenous esmolol and lidocaine for correcting arrhythmias; ventilator positive pressure ventilation for respiratory support; plasmapheresis when electrocardiogram monitoring showed ventricular arrhythmia storms; electrical cardioversion, tracheal intubation, and cardiopulmonary resuscitation. The case aims to raise awareness of immune-mediated cardiotoxicity and bring thoughts to the prospects of immunotherapy in thymoma.",
  "summary": "A 45-year-old female thymoma patient developed fatal immune checkpoint inhibitor-induced myocarditis 14 days after sintilimab despite high-dose methylprednisolone and escalation to immunoglobulin, antiarrhythmics, ventilation, and plasmapheresis."
}

