  "other_facts": "Objective: to evaluate adherence to the recommendations of the EXTRIP (Extracorporeal Treatments In Poisoning) group for the indication of renal replacement therapy in patients with lithium poisoning, determine factors associated with use of this therapeutic method, and analyze its impact on clinical outcomes. The study included all episodes of lithium poisoning treated from 2015 through 2022 in emergency departments and clinical toxicology units of 23 tertiary referral centers in Spain, Ireland, and Switzerland. Events were retrospectively classified according to EXTRIP criteria as RRT recommended, suggested, or not recommended, and adherence and associations with clinical outcomes were analyzed using uni- and multivariate analyses. Independent predictors for performing RRT were peak lithium concentration 3.45 (SD 1.53) vs 2.05 (SD 0.71) mEq/L (P < .001), decreased level of consciousness 49 (41.2%) vs 92 (21.4%) (P < .001), and presence of atrioventricular block 6 (5.0%) vs 5 (1.2%) (P = .008). Adherence to EXTRIP recommendations was moderate to high but was not associated with improved clinical outcomes; the findings support an individualized approach based on clinical assessment when indicating RRT and suggest the need to refine EXTRIP criteria to improve applicability in real-world practice.",
