  "other_facts": "Atherosclerotic diseases, like coronary artery disease, are recognized to be associated with oxidative stress and inflammation. Sestrin2 is a stress-inducible protein with anti-oxidant and anti-inflammatory properties. Plasma sestrin2 levels were previously reported to be high in patients with coronary artery disease, but no study had shown their prognostic value in patients with coronary artery disease. Plasma sestrin2 was higher in CAD than non-CAD patients (P < 0.05). Patients with MACE had higher sestrin2 levels and more often had sestrin2 > 15.0 ng/mL than those without MACE (P < 0.001). Kaplan-Meier analysis showed lower event-free survival for sestrin2 > 15.0 ng/mL versus <= 15.0 ng/mL (P < 0.001). In the overall cohort, sestrin2 > 15.0 ng/mL predicted MACE independently of CAD and atherosclerotic risk factors. Among CAD patients, sestrin2 > 15.0 ng/mL predicted MACE independently of CAD severity and coronary revascularization.",
