  "other_facts": "Acute gastroenteritis is a common cause of pediatric emergency department visits and is frequently associated with dehydration and metabolic disturbances. Rapid assessment of electrolytes and glucose is essential in clinical management. Venous blood gas analysis provides faster results than standard serum biochemistry, but its agreement with serum biochemistry in pediatric acute gastroenteritis remained unclear. The study included children who presented to the pediatric emergency department with acute gastroenteritis between January 1, 2024, and December 31, 2025. A total of 1853 paired measurements were obtained from 1191 patients because some children contributed multiple paired measurements during repeated testing. Method comparison was performed according to Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute EP09-A3 guidelines using intraclass correlation coefficients, paired comparisons, Pearson correlation, and Bland-Altman analysis. Venous blood gas electrolyte and glucose measurements are not interchangeable with serum biochemistry in children with acute gastroenteritis. Blood gas analysis may be useful for rapid screening or trend monitoring in urgent settings, but confirmatory serum biochemical testing remains necessary for clinical decision-making in pediatric acute gastroenteritis.",
