  "other_facts": "Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy is not a common complication of pregnancy but may threaten fetal condition; elevated bile acids define intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy and determine its severity; elevated ALT and AST are indicators of hepatocyte damage; fetal condition in intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy depends on the degree of liver damage; the most common complication is preterm delivery; erythropoietin is a peptide hormone produced mostly in the kidneys and liver due to tissue hypoxia; erythropoietin concentration in blood serum of pregnant women increases because of placental production; reduced blood flow through the fetal-placental unit causes fetal complications associated with intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy; elevated total bile acid concentration is the reason for decreased blood flow; the study hypothesis was that elevated bile acids reduce blood flow through the fetal-placental unit, causing placental hypoxia and increased placental erythropoietin secretion; the aim was to find a correlation between high bile acid levels and erythropoietin concentration in serum of women with intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy without anemia and renal dysfunction and to evaluate pregnancy course and outcome; the study was conducted at the Department of Obstetrics and Gynaecology, Institute of Mother and Child in Warsaw; 73 pregnant women were included, with 33 in the intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy group and 40 in the control group; inclusion criteria for the intrahepatic cholestasis group were total bile acids >=11 μmol/L, ALT >41 U/L and/or AST >40 U/L, and pruritus current or history; exclusion criteria were anemia with hemoglobin <11 g/dL, viral hepatitis A, B, or C, other liver or biliary tract abnormalities, alcohol addiction, drug addiction, HIV infection, skin diseases with itching and rash, acute and chronic kidney disease, bone disease, acute and chronic bleeding in pregnancy, and preeclampsia; laboratory analysis of liver function, kidney function, and blood counts was performed on the same day as total bile acid and erythropoietin concentration measurement; pruritus intensity was assessed using a 5-degree scale proposed by the author; fetal condition was monitored during laboratory testing and delivery with cardiotocography and ultrasound; newborn analysis used gestational age at delivery, birth weight, 1-minute Apgar score, 5-minute Apgar score, and umbilical artery blood gas parameters; statistical analysis was performed using Statistica 5.5 PL; the authors concluded that there was no correlation between bile acid concentration and erythropoietin in intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy, and that preterm delivery and worse neonatal outcome were more common in the intrahepatic cholestasis group, indicating need for improved perinatal care and further studies",
  "summary": "In 73 pregnant women, intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy was associated with higher bile acid levels, more preterm birth, and worse neonatal outcomes, but no correlation was found between bile acid concentration and erythropoietin concentration."
}

