  "other_facts": "Numerous cellular and animal studies demonstrated that redox-active Mn(III) N-alkyl- and N-alkoxyalkylpyridyporphyrins (MnPs) can protect normal tissue while suppressing tumor growth. The mechanism primarily involves modulation of NF-κB and Nrf2 signaling pathways via catalysis of MnP/H2O2-driven protein thiol oxidation. Mn porphyrins, commonly known as mimics of superoxide dismutase, have entered clinical trials and represent a new line of therapeutics affecting cellular redox status/oxidative stress rather than specific proteins. BMX-001 is the most clinically advanced Mn porphyrin and has progressed into five Phase II clinical trials, two of which are related to central nervous system injuries. No efficient treatment for chemotherapy-induced neuropathy is currently available. BMX-001 was assessed for its effect on paclitaxel-induced neuropathy. Dosing included a clinically relevant subcutaneous regimen. Previously, strong anticancer effects of Mn porphyrins were seen only when combined with radiation, chemotherapy, and ascorbate. High-grade serous ovarian cancer is the first in vivo cancer studied in which a redox-active Mn porphyrin as a single agent exhibits strong anticancer effect comparable to paclitaxel. The effect is presumably due to high tumor levels of BMX-001 and high oxidative stress specific to the aggressive chemoresistant CAOV2 cell line. The strong anticancer effect of BMX-001 would allow lowering paclitaxel dosing and reducing neuropathy. The combined neuropathy protection and anticancer efficacy demonstrates therapeutic potential for gynecological cancers and may be relevant for all cancers treated with neuropathy-inducing chemotherapeutics.",
