  "other_facts": "Reported values are mean ± SEM. Background: plant sterol cholesterol-lowering benefits are hampered by large heterogeneity across individuals, potentially because of genetic polymorphisms. Objective: to investigate the impact of candidate genetic variations on cholesterol response to plant sterols in a trial recruiting individuals with high or low endogenous cholesterol synthesis estimated by lathosterol to cholesterol ratio. Study procedures: participants consumed plant sterols or placebo for 28 days in a dual-center, single-blind, randomized crossover design; cholesterol synthesis and change in cholesterol absorption were measured with stable isotopic tracers; candidate single-nucleotide polymorphisms and apolipoprotein E isoform were assessed by TaqMan genotyping assay. Cholesterol fractional synthesis rate was higher in participants with high endogenous cholesterol synthesis than in those with low endogenous cholesterol synthesis (P < 0.001). Low-density lipoprotein cholesterol lowering in response to plant sterols was associated with individuals' genotypes. CYP7A1-rs3808607 T/T homozygotes showed no low-density lipoprotein cholesterol lowering (P = 0.9999, n = 20); the presence of the G allele was associated with a dose-dependent low-density lipoprotein cholesterol response (G/T P = 0.0006, n = 35; G/G P = 0.0009, n = 8). APOE ε3 carriers responded less than APOE ε4 carriers (APOE ε3 P = 0.0370, n = 40; APOE ε4 P < 0.0001, n = 23). The combined CYP7A1-rs3808607 T/T/APOE ε3 genoset was associated with nonresponsiveness (P = 0.9999, n = 14). rs5882 in cholesteryl ester transfer protein and rs4148217 in ATP-binding cassette subfamily G member 8 did not associate with low-density lipoprotein cholesterol lowering. Cholesterol absorption decreased as a result of plant sterol consumption, but this decrease was not related to circulating low-density lipoprotein cholesterol concentrations, cholesterol synthesis phenotype, or genotypes. Conclusion: CYP7A1-rs3808607 and apolipoprotein E isoform are associated with the extent of reduction in circulating low-density lipoprotein cholesterol in response to plant sterol consumption and could serve as potential predictive genetic markers to identify individuals who would derive maximum low-density lipoprotein cholesterol lowering with plant sterol consumption. Trial registration: clinicaltrials.gov NCT01131832.",
  "summary": "In mildly hypercholesterolemic adults in a 28-day randomized crossover trial, plant sterol-induced LDL cholesterol lowering varied by CYP7A1-rs3808607 genotype and APOE isoform, with T/T and APOE ε3 carriers showing reduced or absent response."
