  "other_facts": "Background: elevated CSF concentrations of CRH and norepinephrine are present in patients with PTSD, and the effects of traumatic stimuli on these CSF stress-related hormones were unknown. Each patient underwent 6-h continuous lumbar CSF withdrawal on two occasions, 6-9 weeks apart. The traumatic film was a 1-h film containing combat footage; the neutral film was a 1-h film on how to oil paint. CRH and norepinephrine were quantified in CSF and ACTH and cortisol in plasma at 10-min intervals before, during, and after symptom provocation. Subjective anxiety and mood were monitored using 100-mm visual analog scales. Blood pressure and heart rate were obtained every 10 min from a left leg monitor. Eight of 10 patients completed two CSF withdrawal procedures. The CSF norepinephrine increase was postulated, while the CRH decline was surprising. The authors concluded that CSF norepinephrine and CRH differentially change after 1 h of trauma-related audiovisual stimulation in chronic combat-related PTSD; the CRH decline could reflect increased uptake of CSF CRH into brain tissue, increased utilization, increased degradation, or acute stress-related inhibition/suppression of CRH secretion.",
