  "other_facts": "Idiopathic osteoarthritis is the most common form of osteoarthritis worldwide and remains the leading cause of disability and associated socio-economic burden in an increasing aging population; traditionally osteoarthritis has been viewed as a degenerative joint disease characterized by progressive destruction of articular cartilage and changes in subchondral bone culminating in joint failure; osteoarthritis etiology is multifactorial involving genetic, mechanical, and environmental factors; treatment modalities include analgesia, joint injection with steroids or hyaluronic acid, oral supplements including glucosamine and chondroitin sulfate, and physiotherapy; there is significant interest in discovery of disease modifying agents; glucosamine is commonly prescribed even though therapeutic efficacy and mechanism of action remain controversial; inflammatory cytokines including IL-1β and proteinases such as MMP-13 have been implicated in pathogenesis and progression of osteoarthritis together with associated CpG demethylation in their promoters; the study investigated the potential of GlcN to modulate NF-kB activity and cytokine-induced abnormal gene expression in articular chondrocytes and whether this is associated with an epigenetic process; chondrocytes were cultured for 5 weeks in six separate groups: control culture; 2.5 ng/ml IL-1β plus 2.5 ng/ml oncostatin M; 2 mM N-acetyl GlcN from Sigma-Aldrich; 2.5 ng/ml IL-1β plus 2.5 ng/ml oncostatin M plus 2 mM GlcN; 1.0 μM BAY 11-7082, an NF-kB inhibitor, from Calbiochem, Darmstadt, Germany; and 2.5 ng/ml IL-1β plus 2.5 ng/ml oncostatin M plus 1.0 μM BAY; IL1B and MMP13 mRNA levels were examined using qRT-PCR; percentage DNA methylation in CpG sites of the IL1β and MMP13 proximal promoters was quantified by pyrosequencing; GlcN and BAY alone had negligible effect on methylation status of the IL1B promoter; IL-1β plus oncostatin M treatment increased MMP13 mRNA levels independently of GlcN or BAY and no