    "design": "Post-hoc analysis of the Hypertension Intervention Nurse Telemedicine Study (HINTS) clinical trial",
    "year": null,
    "sample_size": 573,
    "follow_up": "Assessments at 6, 12, and 18 months"
  },
  "population": {
    "age": null,
    "age_criteria": null,
    "sex": null,
    "weight": null,
    "key_characteristics": "573 primary care patients: 284 African American and 289 non-Hispanic white; interventions were activated based on poorly controlled home blood pressure values"
  },
  "intervention": {
    "name": "Combined behavioral management and medication management intervention; trial arms also included nurse-administered, physician-directed medication management alone, nurse-administered behavioral management alone, and usual care",
    "dose": null,
    "frequency": null,
    "duration": null
  },
  "outcomes": [
    {
      "measure": "Change in mean systolic blood pressure post-baseline, African American patients in combined intervention versus African American patients in usual care",
      "timepoint": "12 months",
      "effect": "6.6 mmHg greater improvement",
      "ci": "95% CI -12.5 to -0.7; p=0.03"
    },
    {
      "measure": "Change in mean systolic blood pressure post-baseline, African American patients in combined intervention versus African American patients in usual care",
      "timepoint": "18 months",
      "effect": "9.7 mmHg greater improvement",
      "ci": "95% CI -16.0 to -3.4; p=0.003"
    },
    {
      "measure": "Change in mean diastolic blood pressure, African American patients in combined intervention versus African American patients in usual care",
      "timepoint": "18 months",
      "effect": "4.8 mmHg lower",
      "ci": "95% CI -8.5 to -1.0; p=0.01"
    }
  ],
  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "African Americans are significantly more likely than whites to have uncontrolled hypertension, contributing to disparities in cardiovascular disease and events. Study arms were: 1) nurse-administered, physician-directed medication management intervention utilizing a validated clinical decision support system; 2) nurse-administered behavioral management intervention; 3) combined behavioral management and medication management intervention; 4) usual care. General linear models (PROC MIXED in SAS, version 9.2) estimated predicted means at 6-month, 12-month, and 18-month time points by intervention arm and race subgroups, with separate models for systolic and diastolic blood pressure. There were no analogous differences for non-Hispanic white patients. The combination of home blood pressure monitoring, remote medication management, and telephone tailored behavioral self-management appears particularly effective for improving blood pressure among African Americans, with no effect seen among non-Hispanic white patients.",
  "summary": "In a post-hoc analysis of the HINTS trial, a combined home blood pressure monitoring, remote medication management, and telephone behavioral intervention significantly improved blood pressure among African American patients but not among non-Hispanic white patients."
}

