  "other_facts": "Myocardial siderosis in thalassemia major remains the leading cause of death in developing countries; once heart failure develops, the outlook is usually poor with precipitous deterioration and death. Cardiovascular magnetic resonance can measure cardiac iron deposition directly using the magnetic relaxation time T2*, allowing earlier diagnosis and treatment and helping reduce mortality from this cardiac affection. The study aimed to determine the prevalence of cardiac siderosis in heavily iron-loaded Egyptian patients and its relation to liver iron concentration, serum ferritin, and left ventricular ejection fraction. Tissue iron levels were determined by CMR with cardiac T2* and liver R2*. Myocardial siderosis was defined as T2* <20 ms, and severe cardiac siderosis was defined as T2* <10 ms. The authors concluded that there was a low prevalence of myocardial siderosis in Egyptian thalassemia patients despite very high serum ferritin and high liver iron concentration; T2* is the best test to identify at-risk patients who can be managed with optimization of chelation therapy; and a genetic component for resistance to cardiac iron loading in this population should be considered.",
  "summary": "In 89 heavily iron-loaded Egyptian β-thalassemia patients receiving chelation therapy, CMR T2* identified myocardial siderosis in 24.7% and severe cardiac siderosis in 7.9%, with no significant correlations between cardiac T2* and age, left ventricular ejection fraction, liver iron concentration, or serum ferritin."
