  "other_facts": "Background: gut microbiome plays an important role in disease development; microbiome composition is influenced by mode of delivery at birth, diet, and antibiotic use; environmental chemical exposures are largely unknown; triclosan is an antimicrobial compound found in many personal care products and widely detected in human urine; enterolactone is an intestinal metabolite produced via bacterial transformation of dietary lignans from seeds and nuts and has known susceptibility to oral antibiotics. Methods: urinary triclosan and enterolactone were examined in 2005-2008 U.S. National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey subjects aged >=20 years (n=3041); the association between prescription antibiotic use and enterolactone was examined to confirm susceptibility to changes in bacterial composition; associations between natural log-transformed enterolactone and triclosan detected vs not detected (<2.3 ng/mL), triclosan quintiles Q1-Q5, and any vs no antibiotics were estimated with multiple linear regression; models adjusted for sex, age, race, body mass index, poverty income ratio, education, fiber intake, bowel movement frequency, cotinine, and creatinine (n=2441). Results: triclosan was detected in 80% of subjects (range <2.3-3620 ng/mL); enterolactone was detected in >99% of subjects (range <0.1-122,000 ng/mL); Q5 threshold was >=104.5 ng/mL; any antibiotic use n=112; antibiotic association was driven by inverse associations with macrolide derivatives, quinolones, sulfonamides, and lincomycin derivatives; no sex-specific effects were observed for the antibiotic association. Conclusions: antibiotics, but not triclosan, are negatively associated with urinary enterolactone; antibiotics may reduce enterolactone by killing certain gut bacteria; at levels detected in the U.S., triclosan does not appear to act similarly despite broad antimicrobial properties; additional study of determinants of triclosan exposure and enterolactone production may be needed to better understand positive associations among women.",
  "summary": "In a cross-sectional analysis of 3041 U.S. adults from NHANES 2005-2008, urinary triclosan was not associated with urinary enterolactone overall, whereas prescription antibiotic use was associated with lower enterolactone, with a positive triclosan-enterolactone association observed in women."
}

