  "other_facts": "Both yaws and trachoma are endemic in the Pacific. Mass treatment with azithromycin is the mainstay of the WHO strategy for both the eradication of yaws and the elimination of trachoma as a public health problem. The dose recommended for trachoma is lower than that for yaws. In countries where both diseases are endemic, there is potential for synergy between yaws and trachoma control programs if mass treatment with the lower dose of azithromycin is effective for yaws. In an earlier study, a single round of mass treatment with azithromycin 20 mg/kg undertaken for trachoma elimination produced a profound reduction in the clinical and serological prevalence of yaws. The benefit of a single round of mass treatment with azithromycin 20 mg/kg appeared to extend to 18 months without any further intervention. The lack of a significant change in seroprevalence from 6 to 18 months after mass treatment suggests that interventions could be spaced at yearly intervals without a significant loss of impact and might facilitate integration of yaws eradication with other neglected tropical disease control programmes. Mass drug administration coverage above 90% was associated with significantly better outcomes than coverages lower than this threshold; strategies to improve coverage at all stages of yaws eradication efforts should be investigated.",
