  "other_facts": "HELIUS study. Isolated systolic hypertension of the young has been associated with both normal and increased cardiovascular risk, attributed to differences in central systolic blood pressure and arterial stiffness. The highest prevalence of isolated systolic hypertension was in individuals of African descent. Subjects with isolated systolic hypertension had lower central systolic blood pressure and pulse wave velocity compared with isolated diastolic or systolic-diastolic hypertension, resembling values observed in subjects with high-normal blood pressure. They had lower augmentation index and larger stroke volume compared with all other hypertensive phenotypes. In isolated systolic hypertension, increased systolic blood pressure amplification was associated with male gender, Dutch origin, lower age, taller stature, lower augmentation index, and larger stroke volume. Isolated systolic hypertension of the young is heterogeneous; average central systolic blood pressure values are comparable to high-normal blood pressure, while individual levels may be normal or raised. Measurement of central systolic blood pressure may aid in discriminating high from low cardiovascular risk.",
