  "other_facts": "Bone-modifying agents such as bisphosphonates and denosumab are usually administered every 4 weeks (standard) to prevent skeletal-related events; every-12-week dosing is the de-escalated comparator. Recent randomized controlled trials suggest every-12-week dosing may be non-inferior. The review compared efficacy and harms of every-4-week and every-12-week bone-modifying agent dosing in patients with bone metastases from breast cancer. Searches included Medline, PubMed, and the Cochrane Register of Controlled Trials from 1947 to March 14, 2018 and conference abstracts from 2014 to March 14, 2018. Meta-analyses were performed using PRISMA guidelines and random-effects models, with findings reported as risk ratios with 95% confidence intervals. From 1311 citations, 8 full-text articles and 1 abstract were identified, comprising data from 5 completed randomized clinical trials (n=1807). Zoledronate every 12 weeks had similar efficacy to every 4 weeks. Results did not differ whether patients had received prior intravenous bisphosphonate. De-escalation was associated with a non-statistically significant lower risk of increased creatinine. There are insufficient data for pamidronate and denosumab de-escalation. The data are supportive of de-escalation of zoledronate from onset.",
  "summary": "This systematic review and meta-analysis of 5 randomized clinical trials involving 1807 patients with bone metastases from breast cancer found that every-12-week zoledronate had similar efficacy to every-4-week dosing and may be associated with lower risk of increased creatinine, supporting zoledronate de-escalation."
