  "other_facts": "Botulinum toxin injections are effective in relieving focal spasticity resulting from upper motor neuron injuries. U.S. approved doses for onabotulinumtoxinA and incobotulinumtoxinA are up to 400 units, yet higher doses are often used. The study objective was to determine differences in adverse event risk using higher (>600 units) compared with lower doses within clinically applicable categories, differences between botulinum toxin-A types, and whether cervical muscle injection is associated with increased adverse event risk. The setting was a freestanding rehabilitation hospital network. Interventions were reported as not applicable. Main outcome measures were adverse events divided into dose ranges ≤400 units, 401-600 units, or >600 units. Of 889 injections in 342 patients, 65% were ≤400 units, 21% were 401-600 units, and 14% were >600 units. Level of evidence was III. Conclusion: adverse event risk was not significantly increased with ona- and/or incobotulinumtoxinA doses up to 600 units, suggesting the current 400-unit approved upper bound may need reexamination; doses above 600 units increased adverse effects and clinical benefit versus risk should be considered.",
