  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Current athlete-specific protein recommendations are based almost exclusively on research in males. The minimally invasive indicator amino acid oxidation technique was used to determine the daily protein intake that maximizes whole-body protein synthesis and net protein balance after exercise in strength-trained females. Steady-state whole-body phenylalanine rate of appearance, oxidation (reciprocal of protein synthesis), and net protein balance (protein synthesis minus rate of appearance) were determined from oral [C] phenylalanine ingestion. Constant phenylalanine intake was 30.5 mg·kg·d and excess tyrosine intake was 40.0 mg·kg·d. Total protein oxidation was estimated from urinary urea-creatinine ratio. A mixed model biphase linear regression was used; correlations were r = 0.64 for the oxidation breakpoint and r = 0.65 for the net protein balance breakpoint. The breakpoints indicate saturation in whole-body anabolism. The safe protein intake (upper 95% confidence interval) to maximize anabolism and minimize protein oxidation during early ~8-h postexercise recovery is at the upper end of American College of Sports Medicine athlete recommendations, i.e. 1.2-2.0 g·kg·d.",
  "summary": "In eight resistance-trained females during early postexercise recovery, protein intake breakpoints maximizing whole-body anabolism were approximately 1.49-1.53 g·kg·d, with safe intake at the upper end of the 1.2-2.0 g·kg·d athlete recommendation range."
}

