  "other_facts": "Lung ultrasound has been widely used in the diagnosis and differential diagnosis of neonatal lung diseases, but whether it can replace routine chest X-ray in neonatal intensive care units remains controversial. The authors summarized their neonatal intensive care unit clinical practice over the past 3 years to explore the feasibility and necessity of using lung ultrasound instead of chest X-ray. Clinical data and lung ultrasound examination results from 1,381 newborn infants with respiratory difficulty hospitalized from March 2017 to February 2020 were retrospectively collected to analyze the types of lung diseases diagnosed and the reliability of lung ultrasound. Among the 1,381 infants, 17 patients with respiratory distress were confirmed as having severe heart disease by echocardiography, while the remaining 1,364 patients had lung diseases: pneumonia 697 patients (51.1%), respiratory distress syndrome 251 patients (17.4%), transient tachypnea of the newborn 197 patients (13.3%), atelectasis 89 patients (5.6%), pneumothorax 46 patients (3.2%), pulmonary hemorrhage 69 patients (4.5%), severe pleural effusion 18 patients (1.32%), congenital pulmonary sequestration 3 patients (0.22%), bullae of the lung 2 patients (0.15%), and congenital cystic adenomatoid malformation 2 patients. The authors concluded that 3-year clinical practice experience indicated lung ultrasound could completely replace chest X-ray for diagnosis and differential diagnosis of neonatal lung diseases in the neonatal intensive care unit, and that lung ultrasound had higher accuracy and reliability than X-ray.",
  "summary": "In a retrospective 3-year neonatal intensive care unit study of 1,381 newborn infants with respiratory difficulty, lung ultrasound was considered feasible and potentially able to replace chest X-ray for diagnosing neonatal lung diseases, while chest X-ray had a combined missed diagnosis and misdiagnosis rate of 26.3% among 217 infants imaged before admission."
