      "event": "Major cardiac event resulting in death",
      "rate": "1 patient"
    }
  ],
  "other_facts": "Management of radiation-induced ureteral stricture is complex, requiring chronic drainage or morbid definitive open reconstruction. The study reviewed a multi-institutional robotic ureteral reconstruction database between January 2013 and January 2020 and identified patients with radiation-induced ureteral strictures. A total of 32 patients with 35 ureteral units underwent robotic ureteral reconstruction. Five major reconstruction techniques were utilized: end-to-end ureteral reimplantation (anastomosing the bladder to the transected ureter), side-to-side ureteral reimplantation (anastomosing the bladder to an anterior ureterotomy proximal to the stricture without ureteral transection), buccal or appendiceal mucosa graft ureteroplasty, appendiceal bypass graft, and ileal ureter interposition. Technique distribution was end-to-end reimplantation 21 (60.0%), side-to-side reimplantation 8 (22.9%), appendiceal procedure 4 (11.4%), buccal mucosa graft ureteroplasty 1 (2.9%), and ileal ureter interposition 1. Adjunctive procedures were performed when necessary for mobility (psoas hitch) and improved vascularity (omental wrap). Median operative time was 215 minutes (IQR 177-281), median estimated blood loss was 100 mL (IQR 50-150), and median length of stay was 2 days (IQR 1-3). One patient who died from a major cardiac event was excluded from follow-up calculations. The authors concluded that robotic ureteral reconstruction can be performed in patients with radiation-induced ureteral strictures with excellent outcomes, that surgeons must be prepared to perform adjunctive procedures for mobility and improved vascularity due to poor tissue quality, and that repeat procedures for radiation-induced ureteral strictures heighten the risk of necrosis and failure."
}

