  "other_facts": "ISNCSCI is International Standards for the Neurologic Classification of SCI; ISAFSCI is International Standards to document remaining Autonomic Function after SCI. Background: low blood pressure may lead to reduced CBFv during orthostatic challenge in newly injured traumatic SCI and may relate to neurological level of injury or cardiovascular autonomic impairment. Objective: to examine the influence of patient demographics, ISNCSCI scores, and ISAFSCI scores on hemodynamic responses to a bedside sit-up test. The modified sit-up test was conducted at the bedside with hips at 45° to 90° and legs horizontal, level with hips; heart rate, blood pressure, and CBFv were recorded for 10 minutes in supine and seated positions. Overall, 28/53 (53%) patients met ISAFSCI criteria. Conclusion: total ISAFSCI score, neurological level of injury, and sensory scores were positively associated with orthostatic changes in CBFv. Long-term follow-up should be considered to determine consequences of diminished CBFv on cognitive function and quality of life after SCI.",
