      "effect": null,
      "ci": null
    }
  ],
  "safety": [
    {
      "event": "hypoxemia",
      "rate": null
    }
  ],
  "other_facts": "Endotracheal intubation is lifesaving but carries a high risk of adverse events, notably hypoxemia. Preoxygenation is performed before introducing the tube to increase the safe apnea time. Oxygenation is monitored by pulse oximeter measurement of peripheral oxygen saturation (SpO2). SpO2 is unreliable at the high oxygenation levels produced by preoxygenation and, in the event of desaturation, may not decrease sufficiently early to allow preventive measures. The oxygen reserve index (ORI) is a dimensionless parameter that can be measured continuously by a fingertip monitor and reflects oxygenation in the moderate hyperoxia range. ORI ranges from 0 to 1 when arterial oxygen saturation (PaO2) varies between 100 and 200 mmHg, as occurs during preoxygenation. No trial has assessed the potential effects of ORI monitoring to guide preoxygenation for endotracheal intubation in unstable patients. Control group: preoxygenation is guided by SpO2 values recorded by a standard pulse oximeter, according to standard of care, with the goal of obtaining 100% SpO2 during preoxygenation, and preoxygenation lasts at least 3 minutes. The standard-of-care endotracheal intubation technique is used in both arms. Baseline parameters, rapid-sequence induction medications, endotracheal intubation devices, and physiological data are recorded. Statistical assumptions: 10% standard deviation for the lowest SpO2 value in the control group; no missing data; crossover of 5% of patients; bilateral alpha risk set at 0.05; including 950 patients provides 85% power for detecting a 2% between-group absolute difference in the lowest SpO2 value. If ORI monitoring with a target of >=0.6 is found to increase the lowest SpO2 value during endotracheal intubation, this trial may change current practice regarding preoxygenation for endotracheal intubation. Trial registration: ClinicalTrials.gov NCT05867875, registered April 27, 2023.",
  "summary": "A multicenter randomized open-label superiority trial in 950 critically ill adults requiring endotracheal intubation will compare ORI-guided preoxygenation with standard SpO2-guided preoxygenation to determine whether ORI monitoring increases the lowest SpO2 value during intubation."
}

