  "other_facts": "Background: broad use of bedaquiline and pretomanid as the mainstay of new tuberculosis regimens is a risk due to increasing bedaquiline resistance; BTZ-043 is a first-in-class DprE1 inhibitor with strong bactericidal activity in murine models. Study conducted in two specialised tuberculosis sites in Cape Town, South Africa. Stage 1 was a multiple-ascending dose phase 1b with an adaptive continual reassessment method; starting dose 250 mg; planned dose increments of 250 mg up to 2000 mg; cohorts of three participants enrolled sequentially. Stage 2 was a phase 2a dose-expansion stage; participants were randomly assigned 3:3:3:2 using sealed opaque envelopes to receive one of three oral BTZ-043 doses decided after stage 1 or standard of care (isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol). BTZ-043 groups also received oral dolutegravir in a third of participants or a probe drug cocktail in two-thirds of participants containing caffeine (CYP1A2), tolbutamide (CYP2C9), dextromethorphan (CYP2D6), midazolam (CYP3A4), and digoxin (P-glycoprotein). Study staff and participants were not masked; laboratory staff were masked to treatment assignment. Primary outcome was safety and tolerability over 14 days of dosing by adverse events in the safety analysis population. Secondary outcomes were bactericidal activity measured by time to positivity and colony-forming unit count; pharmacokinetics in stage 2 including food effect; and drug-drug interactions with CYP450 enzymes, P-glycoprotein, and dolutegravir. ClinicalTrials.gov NCT04044001 (completed). Stage 1: 61 patients assessed for eligibility and 24 enrolled into seven dose cohorts between Nov 13, 2019 and Aug 13, 2020; dose escalations were performed safely up to 1750 mg of BTZ-043 with three participants per dose cohort and two dose cohorts for the highest dose. Stage 2: 151 patients assessed for eligibility and 54 enrolled and randomly assigned between Feb 2, 2021 and Feb 9, 2022 to receive 250, 500, and 1000 mg of BTZ-043 or standard of care. Bioequivalence was defined as the 90% CI of the AUC0-infinity geometric mean ratio from administration to day 14 entirely within 80 to 125%. BTZ-043total comprises BTZ-043 plus metabolite 2. Interpretation: based on a small sample size, BTZ-043 is a promising antituberculosis drug candidate with favourable safety and good bactericidal activity; larger follow-up studies are needed to detect less frequent safety signals, further explore drug-drug interactions, identify the best dose, and evaluate efficacy in combination with other drugs. Funding: EDCTP2 programme; German Ministry for Education and Research; German Center for Infection Research; InfectControl; Bavarian Ministry for Science and the Arts; Swiss State Secretariat for Education, Research, and Innovation; Nederlandse Organisatie voor Wetenschappelijk Onderzoek.",
