  "other_facts": "Background: surgery in NF2-related schwannomatosis vestibular schwannoma carries higher risk of facial nerve damage, hearing loss, and partial resection than sporadic cases; radiosurgery is associated with higher failure compared with sporadic schwannomas; bevacizumab is frequently considered in NF2-related schwannomatosis, but some patients progress despite treatment; hemorrhage and impaired healing are specific surgical considerations in bevacizumab-treated patients. Manufacturers recommend stopping bevacizumab 6 to 8 weeks preoperatively. Study aim: assess perioperative bleeding risk and postoperative outcomes in NF2-related schwannomatosis vestibular schwannoma patients undergoing vestibular schwannoma surgery after preoperative bevacizumab. Methods: retrospective analysis of medical and surgical records and imaging reviews from 4 high-volume tertiary academic referral centers for NF2-related schwannomatosis and vestibular schwannoma. Bevacizumab was stopped at a mean of 5.8 ± 4.0 months before surgery. Conclusion: a bevacizumab-free window between 6 weeks and 6 months before tumor resection seems optimal, depending on the clinical scenario, particularly for patients with high cumulative exposure.",
