  "other_facts": "Long-term renal consequences of acromegaly remain poorly characterized. The study assessed prevalence and independent predictors of renal cysts, chronic kidney disease, and frequency of urinary-system malignancies in a large acromegaly cohort. Patients were managed at a single tertiary center over four decades and retrospectively evaluated. Renal cyst and CKD predictors were identified by binary multivariate logistic regression with sensitivity analyses including IGF-1 exposure metrics; eGFR trajectories were analyzed by nested analysis of covariance (ANCOVA); standardized prevalence ratios and incidence ratios were computed against age- and sex-stratified population references. Among CKD patients, preserved eGFR (KDIGO G1-G2) reflected albuminuria- or structure-driven ascertainment. Independent renal cyst predictors were age, nephrolithiasis, liver cysts, and multiple neoplasms; higher baseline potassium inverse association p=0.013. Independent CKD predictors were age, male sex, hypertension, and nephrolithiasis. Cross-sectional and cumulative GH/IGF-1 x ULN did not predict renal cysts or CKD. Cumulative IGF-1 association with follow-up eGFR p=0.022, consistent with hyperfiltration. Age-matched cyst SPRs were significant (both p<0.05); CKD SPR was lower than expected (p=0.002). Urinary-system cancers showed a >7-fold excess (p<0.001). Renal cysts, CKD, and urinary-system malignancies are prevalent in acromegaly; long-term renal burden was predominantly morphological and tubular rather than glomerular; age-matched CKD prevalence was lower than expected while cyst prevalence was elevated; cyst and urinary-cancer excess support dedicated renal surveillance in long-term acromegaly management.",
