  "other_facts": "Antibiotic treatment disturbs gastrointestinal microflora and can cause clinical symptoms, most notably diarrhea; this is especially important in children, for whom antibiotics are prescribed frequently. Previous meta-analyses evaluated probiotics for antibiotic-induced diarrhea in the general population, but optimal pediatric probiotic strains and doses were unclear. The review searched CENTRAL, MEDLINE, EMBASE, CINAHL, and AMED from inception to January 2005, contacted experts, and searched registries and meeting abstracts. Two reviewers independently applied eligibility criteria, assessed methodological quality, extracted data, and analyzed data via standard Cochrane methodology. Six randomized controlled trials were included. Per-protocol heterogeneity was Iota2 = 70.1%; subgroup heterogeneity was Iota2 = 3.5%. Intention-to-treat results were nonsignificant overall. The authors concluded that potential protective effects did not withstand intention-to-treat analysis, recommended further studies with limited losses to follow-up, and suggested trials use Lactobacillus GG, L. sporogens, or Saccharomyces boulardii at 5-40 x 10^9 CFUs daily.",
