  "other_facts": "Separate reference values were recently established for routine blood samples during last trimester pregnancy; previously these were based on blood samples from healthy men or non-pregnant women. Normal changes in variation in steroid hormone levels in the last weeks of pregnancy before delivery are incompletely investigated. The study investigated preterm hormone levels in the search for events leading to increased contractility that might occur in the predelivery weeks and potentially influence the initiation of delivery. Eligibility included at least one previous pregnancy without hypertension or pre-eclampsia; all women (n = 135) had vaginal delivery and spontaneous start of labour. Blood samples were collected during pregnancy weeks 33, 36 and 39 and 1-3 hours postpartum and analysed for serum hCG, oestradiol and progesterone. Postpartum, the values were retrospectively rearranged to correspond with the actual week before the day of delivery. Hormone levels during third-trimester pregnancy have not previously been systematically investigated. Recent data suggest that hCG may have a role as an endogenous tocolytic in normal pregnancy by directly promoting relaxation of uterine contractions. A significant decrease in serum hCG level was found 2-3 weeks before the spontaneous start of labour; this might contribute to increasing contractility in the uterine muscle and gradually initiate the onset of labour.",
