  "other_facts": "A review and meta-analysis by Hamer et al. (2006) showed that a single session of exercise can attenuate post-exercise blood pressure responses to stress; no studies had examined the effects among smokers or with brisk walking. Participants were randomly assigned to a 15-min brisk semi-self-paced walk or passive control condition. After each condition, blood pressure was assessed before and after three psycho-social stressors: computerised Stroop word-colour interference task, speech task, and handling a lit cigarette. A two-way mixed ANCOVA controlled for baseline. Univariate ANCOVAs compared between-groups post-stressor blood pressure while controlling for pre-stressor blood pressure. Post-exercise attenuation effects were moderated by resting blood pressure and self-reported smoking cue-elicited craving; effects were strongest among those with higher blood pressure and smokers who reported typically stronger cravings when faced with smoking cues. Blood pressure responses to the lit cigarette were not associated with responses to the Stroop and speech task. The stress-reducing effect magnitude was similar on average to non-smokers.",
