  "other_facts": "A 29-year-old male with nephrotic syndrome and facial skin erythema was admitted in September 2000 and diagnosed with systemic lupus erythematosus accompanied by lupus nephritis WHO class V. Initial methylprednisolone pulse therapy followed by oral prednisolone decreased disease activity. During prednisolone taper from 60 to 30 mg/day, lupus nephritis flared and he was re-hospitalized in February 2001. Retreatment with methylprednisolone pulse therapy followed by taper from 60 to 30 mg/day was successful, then mizoribine was added as combination therapy. Lupus nephritis flared again after tapering prednisolone to 17.5 mg/day. Mizoribine was changed from 150 mg/day in three divided daily doses to 200 mg/day in two divided daily doses, increasing Cmax from 0.63 to 1.55 microg/ml. At present, prednisolone has been successfully tapered to 7.5 mg/day and remission has been achieved without any side effects. Monitoring serum mizoribine concentration is considered important for effective therapy of systemic lupus erythematosus, especially steroid-resistant lupus nephritis; if serum mizoribine concentration does not reach an effective level, dosage should be adjusted appropriately to maintain adequate serum concentration.",
