  "other_facts": "Lymphoma patients often exhibit abnormal lipid metabolism. Recent evidence suggests that a decrease in circulating high-density lipoprotein cholesterol may occur during lymphomagenesis, reflecting underlying etiology such as inflammation. The study was conducted in the Alpha-Tocopherol Beta-Carotene Cancer Prevention Study cohort. Cox proportional hazards models with age as the underlying time metric were used to estimate relative risks and 95% confidence intervals. There were 201 incident non-Hodgkin lymphoma cases during follow-up. The inverse association between HDL-C and non-Hodgkin lymphoma changed with length of follow-up: 148 cases occurred during the first 10 years and 53 cases occurred during later follow-up. The trend for HDL-C during the first 10 years was significant (P trend < 0.0001). The inverse association was similar for NHL subtypes and was not modified by obesity, blood pressure, physical activity, or alcohol intake, but seemed stronger in men with lower duration of smoking (P interaction = 0.06). The findings implicate HDL-C as a preclinical indicator of NHL and warrant further prospective investigations for its etiologic contribution.",
  "summary": "In a prospective cohort of 27,074 healthy male smokers aged 50 to 69 years, higher prediagnostic HDL-C was associated with lower non-Hodgkin lymphoma risk during the first 10 years but not during later follow-up, suggesting HDL-C may be a preclinical indicator of NHL."
