  "other_facts": "Delayed gastric emptying was defined as gastric emptying coefficient (GEC) less than 3.20 and/or gastric half-emptying time (t50) more than 140 minutes. On univariate analysis, gastric emptying correlated significantly with older age, higher admission APACHE II scores, longer length of stay in ICU prior to gastric emptying measurement, higher respiratory rate, higher FiO2, and higher serum creatinine. The highest occurrence of delayed gastric emptying was observed in patients with head injuries, burns, multi-system trauma, and sepsis. Delayed gastric emptying was least common in patients with myocardial injury and non-gastrointestinal post-operative respiratory failure. Patients with delayed gastric emptying received fewer feeds and stayed longer in ICU and hospital compared to those with normal gastric emptying. The study aimed to evaluate the impact of admission diagnosis on gastric emptying in critically ill patients. Disturbed gastric emptying occurs commonly in critically ill patients. Admission diagnoses are believed to influence the incidence of delayed gastric emptying and subsequent feed intolerance. Patients with burns and head injury are considered to be at greater risk, but the true incidence had not been determined by examination of patient groups of sufficient number. The retrospective review included patient demographics, diagnosis, intensive care unit admission details, gastric emptying, and enteral feeding. Admission diagnosis has a modest impact on gastric emptying in critically ill patients, even after controlling for factors such as age, illness severity, and medication.",
  "summary": "In a retrospective cohort of 132 mechanically ventilated critically ill patients, delayed gastric emptying was common and admission diagnosis had only a modest independent association with gastric emptying after adjustment for severity-related factors."
}

