  "other_facts": "The hallmark of COPD is airflow obstruction, which can develop on the basis of airway disease, emphysema, or both. There are gender differences in the natural history of COPD, which may in part be explained by differences in the pathophysiology of airflow obstruction. The study aimed to determine whether there are gender differences in the severity of CT emphysema among COPD patients. Subjects were enrolled in the National Lung Screening Trial at the University of Alabama at Birmingham and recruited at the time of an annual screening CT examination. Differences between genders were examined, and univariate and multivariate predictors of low attenuation area percentage were determined. Men had more regional and total CT emphysema at all stages of COPD than women. In multivariate regression analysis, only gender (p < 0.001) and FEV(1)/FVC ratio (p < 0.001) predicted total low attenuation area percentage. The difference in radiologic expression may in part explain gender differences in the presentation and natural history of COPD. National Lung Screening Trial registration numbers: NCT00047385 and NCT00047835.",
