  "other_facts": "The attitude of health care personnel is fundamental for organ procurement, especially in transplant-related services. The study assessed attitude toward living kidney donation in a hospital with a cadaveric and living solid organ transplant program. A random sample was stratified by type of service and job category (n=330) in procurement units, transplant units, and follow-up units. The attitude toward cadaveric organ donation was evaluated using a validated psychosocial questionnaire. Statistical analyses included Student's t test, chi(2) test, and logistic regression analysis. The questionnaire completion rate was 94% (n=309). Only two variables were significantly related to attitude toward living kidney donation: age (p=0.013) and willingness to receive a donated living organ if needed (p<0.001); both persisted as significant independent variables in logistic regression. There was a close relationship between attitude toward living kidney donation and attitude toward living liver donation (p<0.001). Personnel in donation and transplantation units tend to have a favorable attitude toward living kidney donation, making them a key element in promoting living kidney donation; youngest workers tend to be more in favor, offering a promising future for living kidney donation, which is becoming more necessary given the cadaveric organ deficit.",
