  "other_facts": "Nonvariceal upper gastrointestinal hemorrhage is a frequent reason for ordinary hospital admission. In Italy, prognostic scores for risk stratification had not been adequately validated, and the impact of the Rockall score on clinical management remained to be established. RING is a register collecting hospital discharge files from gastroenterology units. The analysis included hospital discharge files collected between 2001 and 2005 from 12 gastroenterology units, which issued more than 26,000 ordinary hospital admission discharge files and had been using the Rockall score for defining nonvariceal upper gastrointestinal hemorrhage since 2003. There were no significant changes in age or sex distribution over the years. There were no differences in the distribution of diagnoses before versus after Rockall score introduction. Diagnoses became more accurate after Rockall score introduction, with significantly fewer undefined causes and an increase in peptic ulcer. Mortality declined in parallel with the caselist as a whole. Mean hospital stay, rebleeding, and mortality were correlated with Rockall score severity. The Rockall score was calculated for 1102 ordinary hospital admissions.",
