  "other_facts": "The patient was admitted to hospital due to hyponatremia (sodium 103 mEq/L), which was treated with intravenous infusion of NaCl 3%. Clonazepam had previously been prescribed for anxiety and insomnia and was discontinued. On 2 occasions after initial resolution, clonazepam was discontinued and replaced with alprazolam because the patient believed alprazolam was more beneficial in alleviating anxiety; clonazepam was restarted after recurrences. The authors reported a novel, significant, and sustained improvement in Raynaud's phenomenon and digital ulcers following initiation of clonazepam. As of March 2007, this was reported as the first use of clonazepam in Raynaud's phenomenon and digital ulcer. Raynaud's phenomenon and recurrent digital ulcers are manifestations of vascular disease in patients with systemic sclerosis and lead to pain, impaired function, and tissue loss. Few drugs have previously been shown to affect digital ulcers in the setting of scleroderma. The therapeutic mechanism of clonazepam remains unclear, and clonazepam may offer some advantages compared with current agents; further research is warranted.",
