  "other_facts": "Objective: to assess whether sleep-disordered breathing is a risk factor for metabolic syndrome in overweight children and adolescents and to examine whether sleep-disordered breathing severity is independently associated with glucose intolerance, insulin resistance, and/or dyslipidemia. Study procedures: consecutive overweight or obese subjects underwent polysomnography, fasting blood sample, and oral glucose tolerance test for calculation of area under the curve. Sleep-disordered breathing was defined as respiratory disturbance index >= 2. Metabolic syndrome was present when >= 3 factors were present: waist circumference >= 90th percentile; fasting glucose level >= 110 mg/dL; triglyceride level >= 110 mg/dL; high-density lipoprotein cholesterol level <= 40 mg/dL; blood pressure >= 90th percentile. Multiple regression showed significant associations between SaO2 nadir and high-density lipoprotein cholesterol level, mean SaO2 and both area under the curve glucose and triglyceride levels, and percentage of total sleep time with SaO2 >= 95% and cholesterol level, while controlling for adiposity and sex, puberty, or both. Conclusion: the study supports an interaction between sleep-disordered breathing and metabolic abnormalities, independent of estimates of body fat distribution, in overweight and obese children and adolescents.",
  "summary": "In 104 overweight or obese children and adolescents, sleep-disordered breathing severity markers were independently associated with metabolic syndrome and related metabolic abnormalities."
