  "other_facts": "Guidelines for optimizing type 1 diabetes in young people advocate intensive insulin therapy coupled with personal support from the health care team. Sweet Talk is a novel intervention designed to support patients between clinic visits using text messages sent to a mobile phone. Scheduled messages were tailored to patient profiles and diabetes self-management goals. Generic messages included topical newsletters and anonymized tips from other participants. The system also allowed patients to submit data and questions to the diabetes care team. The aim was to explore how patients with type 1 diabetes interact with the Sweet Talk system to understand its utility. All text messages submitted to Sweet Talk during a 12-month period were recorded. Messaging patterns and content were analyzed using mixed quantitative and qualitative methods. Message content fell into 8 main categories: blood glucose readings, diabetes questions, diabetes information, personal health administration, social messages, technical messages, message errors, and message responses. Unprompted submission of blood glucose values was the most frequent incoming message type. Topical news items generated good responses. Patients used the service to ask questions, submit information about their self-management, and order supplies. Automated scheduled text messaging successfully engaged young people with diabetes. The system was primarily designed to provide push support to patients. Submission of clinical data and queries showed it was seen as a trusted medium for communicating with care providers. Responses to newsletters and submission of personal experiences and tips illustrate potential value for establishing a sense of community. Participants submitted relatively few messages, but positive responses suggest most derived passive support from reading the messages. The Sweet Talk system could be readily adapted to other chronic disease models and age groups, and the results may inform design of future text message support interventions.",
  "summary": "In a 12-month mixed-methods analysis of 64 young people with type 1 diabetes in the intervention arms of a randomized controlled trial, the Sweet Talk automated text-messaging system generated 1180 submitted messages with wide usage variation, high engagement with blood glucose submissions and responses to experience/tip requests, and no reported safety outcomes."
