  "other_facts": "Repetitive seizures can alter regulation of cardiac activity by the autonomic nervous system, and autonomic nervous system dysregulation is thought to be associated with higher morbidity and mortality in epileptic patients, especially sudden unexpected death. Few studies of interictal dysregulation of cardiac activity in children with epilepsy have been performed. A lead I ECG was recorded for 5 min in the interictal period during daylight hours from each subject while awake. Frequency-domain analysis of heart rate variability was performed using a non-parametric fast Fourier transformation. High frequency power was 0.15-0.45 Hz and represented vagal regulation; low frequency power was 0.04-0.15 Hz. LF/(HF+LF) expressed in normalized units (LF%) was considered to mirror sympathetic regulation. The authors postulated that lower heart rate variability resulted from parasympathetic or vagal reduction and suggested that decreased heart rate variability in epileptic children occurs by a different mechanism than in adults with epilepsy.",
  "summary": "In children with refractory generalized epilepsy, interictal heart rate variability showed significantly reduced RR, LF, and HF but unchanged LF%, suggesting reduced vagal/parasympathetic activity."
