  "other_facts": "Medication errors are frequent in a hospital setting and often caused by inappropriate drug handling; systematic prevention strategies are lacking. The study evaluated the prevalence of 20 predefined medication handling errors on the ward. A concurrent questionnaire survey assessed nurses' knowledge of medication handling. Error severity was scored considering prevalence, potential risk of an error, and the involved drug. These scores and the prevalence of corresponding knowledge deficits were used to define the urgency of preventive strategies according to a four-field decision matrix. Main outcome measures were prevalence and potential risk of medication handling errors, corresponding knowledge deficits in nurses committing the errors, and priority of quality improvement. Preparation errors were more frequent than administration errors (P < 0.001). Parenteral drugs were more often involved in errors than enteral drugs (P < 0.001). According to the decision matrix, two enteral drug error types, flaws in light protection and prescribing information, were given maximum priority for quality improvement. For parenteral drugs, five errors appeared most important: incompatibilities, flaws in hygiene, duration of administration, check for visible abnormalities, and prescribing information. The newly developed classification model successfully prioritised medication handling errors for prevention strategies.",
