  "other_facts": "Major Depression with Psychotic Features (psychotic depression) is described as a common, debilitating psychiatric disease. Mifepristone is a cortisol receptor (GRII) antagonist hypothesized to reduce psychotic symptoms in psychotic depression. The trial enrolled patients at 29 sites and randomized them to mifepristone or placebo for 7 days. The primary outcome was assessed at the end of treatment (day 7) and 49 days later (day 56). Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel compared proportions of responders to mifepristone versus placebo adjusting for site. Exploratory analyses compared response of patients with mifepristone plasma concentrations >=1800 ng/ml to placebo. The Breslow-Day test indicated a statistically significant site-by-treatment interaction. Statistical adjustments for site are common in multisite clinical trials, and the study reminds trialists to formally evaluate site-by-treatment interaction. The association between increased mifepristone plasma concentration levels and greater clinical response suggests higher plasma levels may be needed for maximizing the probability of a positive response.",
