  "other_facts": "The study investigated the physical capacity of an NPWT dressing on bacterial growth in an in vitro wound model. Wounds were incubated at 35 degrees C for 6 hours. Bacterial load per gram tissue and per gram polyurethane sponge were measured after 24, 36, and 72 hours. The abstract states that after 24, 36, and 72 hours both models yielded comparable numbers of organisms, with reported P values of 0.081 at 24 hours, 0.455 at 48 hours, and 0.825 at 72 hours. Bacterial load of sponges with or without negative pressure also did not differ. In non-vital tissue without the influence of the immune system, the bacterial load did not decrease in the in vitro model using an NPWT dressing. This observation was independent of the physical effect of continuous negative pressure at 125 mmHg. The reduction in bacteria demonstrated in previous studies appears to be caused by other effects than physical suction alone. The results were limited because non-viable tissue was used, and the effect of suction on dead tissue might be very different from that occurring on perfused tissue, for example in an animal model or in patients.",
