      "ci": null
    },
    {
      "measure": "Cholesterol metabolism markers in insulin-resistant versus insulin-sensitive patients",
      "timepoint": null,
      "effect": "Insulin-resistant patients had higher synthesis markers (desmosterol, lathosterol) and lower absorption markers (cholestanol, sitosterol); the QUICKI-LDL-C correlation ceased to be significant after controlling for these markers",
      "ci": null
    }
  ],
  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Increasing insulin resistance is associated with a shift in cholesterol metabolism to increased synthesis and decreased absorption. Since statins inhibit cholesterol synthesis, the hypothesis was that insulin-resistant patients would have greater LDL-C response to statins than insulin-sensitive patients. Percent LDL-C response to atorvastatin was related to insulin sensitivity using QUICKI. Comparisons were: correlation between percent LDL-C response and QUICKI; differences in cholesterol metabolism markers in insulin-resistant patients (lowest QUICKI tertile) versus insulin-sensitive patients (highest QUICKI tertile); and correlation of QUICKI with percent LDL-C response after correction for cholesterol metabolism markers. The regression effect approximated that reported by pharmacogenomics. The authors concluded that insulin-resistant patients have superior LDL-C responses to statin therapy and that this may be related to increased cholesterol synthesis. Background: patients with features of the metabolic syndrome, e.g. high triglycerides and low HDL-C, may have enhanced benefit from statin therapy. A retrospective analysis from the 4S investigators stratified by HDL-C and triglyceride quartiles revealed variations in statin efficacy. Patients in the lowest HDL-C quartile (<39 mg/dl) and highest triglyceride quartile (>159 mg/dl) had a greater frequency of metabolic syndrome features (high BMI, hypertension, diabetes) than patients in the highest HDL-C quartile (>52 mg/dl) and lowest triglyceride quartile (<98 mg/dl). The 4S investigators suggested enhanced clinical benefit from statins in low HDL-C/high triglyceride patients compared with high HDL-C/low triglyceride patients, with hazard ratios of 0.48 and 0.86 respectively and a treatment-by-subgroup interaction p value of 0.03. The clinical benefit of statin therapy is directly proportional to achieved percent reduction in LDL-C.",
  "summary": "In high-risk vascular patients treated with atorvastatin 80 mg for 6 weeks, lower insulin sensitivity was associated with greater LDL-C reduction, a relationship linked to markers of increased cholesterol synthesis."
}

