  "other_facts": "The novel triazine dendrimers were developed by utilizing differential reactivity of cyanuric chloride (triazine trichloride) to overcome limitations of other dendrimer classes such as toxicity, low yield, and high synthesis cost. They were synthesized by a divergent method using triazine trichloride as the core and diethanolamine as the branching unit, avoiding protecting groups and functional group interconversion up to the third generation. The hydroxyl-terminated dendrimers were characterized by FTIR, 1H NMR, 13C NMR, ES mass spectroscopy, and elemental analysis. The yield of pure G3 dendrimers was 63%. Hemolytic studies were performed. The triazine core is hydrophobic and is supposed to easily incorporate hydrophobic guests, while peripheral hydroxyl groups increase water solubility and reduce toxicity. Potential applications include gene delivery, MRI contrasting agents, vaccines, and use as a solubilization tool.",
