  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Early trauma-induced coagulopathy is debated to reflect disseminated intravascular coagulation with a fibrinolytic phenotype, acute coagulopathy of trauma shock, or other entities. The study investigated the prevalence of overt DIC and ACoTS and characterized these conditions based on biomarker profiles. Patients were stratified according to presence or absence of overt DIC or ACoTS. Overt DIC was defined by International Society of Thrombosis and Hemostasis criteria; ACoTS was defined as activated partial thromboplastin time and/or international normalized ratio above normal reference. Biomarkers analyzed reflected tissue/endothelial cell/glycocalyx damage (histone-complexed DNA fragments, Annexin V, thrombomodulin, syndecan-1), coagulation activation/inhibition (prothrombin fragment 1+2, thrombin/antithrombin complexes, antithrombin, protein C, activated protein C, endothelial protein C receptor, protein S, tissue factor pathway inhibitor, vWF), factor consumption (fibrinogen, FXIII), fibrinolysis (D-dimer, tissue-type plasminogen activator, plasminogen activator inhibitor-1) and inflammation (interleukin-6, terminal complement complex sC5b-9). The authors concluded that ACoTS and non-ACoTS may represent a continuum of coagulopathy reflecting a progressive early evolutionarily adapted hemostatic response to the trauma hit, that both are parts of trauma-induced coagulopathy, and that DIC does not appear to be part of this early response.",
  "summary": "In 80 adult trauma patients sampled early after injury, no overt DIC was found, 15% had ACoTS, and ACoTS was associated with greater injury severity, transfusion requirements, mortality and a biomarker profile of tissue/endothelial/glycocalyx damage, consumption coagulopathy, hyperfibrinolysis and inflammation."
}

