  "other_facts": "Pheochromocytomas are rare but potentially fatal neoplasms; diagnosis and localization can be challenging, and there has been debate regarding the role of adrenal venous sampling. The utility of adrenal venous sampling is hampered by lack of normative adrenal vein catecholamine concentration data and uncertainty about the reliability of lateralization ratios. There was a wide range and wide variation in epinephrine and norepinephrine concentrations in both right and left adrenal veins. Epinephrine and norepinephrine concentrations were statistically significantly higher in the right than left adrenal vein; for epinephrine, the right-to-left difference was up to 83-fold, with P < 0.02. The report provides a reference range for adrenal vein catecholamine concentrations in nonpheochromocytoma patients and concludes that adrenal venous sampling should not be used in the investigation of adrenal pheochromocytoma.",
