  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "The abstract explores two different moments of human cognition: apprehension (A) and judgment (B). Apprehension is a process whereby a coherent perception emerges by recruitment of large neuron groups; it appears as an a-temporal present; its neural correlate is wide synchronization in the EEG gamma band; it may be described as an interpretation of sensorial stimuli in terms of some stored algorithm, via a Bayes procedure. Judgment entails the comparison of two apprehensions acquired at different times, coded in a given language, and retrieved by memory; memory retrieval of different apprehension units coded in a suitable language and comparison of them leads to the formulation of a judgment. Judgment requires self-consciousness, because the judging agent must be well aware that he/she is the same one who faces the two coded apprehensions under scrutiny in order to extract a mutual relation. Unlike apprehension, judgment does not presuppose an algorithm, but rather builds a new behavioural model by an inverse Bayes procedure. This build-up of a novel model is related to creativity and free will.",
  "summary": "This theoretical abstract describes two cognitive moments: apprehension, a approximately 1-second coherent perceptual process associated with EEG gamma synchronization and Bayesian interpretation, and judgment, a approximately 3-second self-conscious comparative process that constructs new behavioral models through inverse Bayes and is related to creativity and free will."
}

