  "other_facts": "Two randomized controlled trials had demonstrated reduced mortality and better neurological outcome in cardiac arrest patients with mild therapeutic hypothermia; before this study, no large observational studies had confirmed the beneficial effects. Data source was the Dutch National Intensive Care Evaluation database. The moment of implementation of mild therapeutic hypothermia for each participating hospital was determined with an Internet survey. Patients were admitted from January 1, 1999 until January 1, 2009. Patients were excluded if they were admitted to an intensive care unit that did not respond to the survey, admitted within 3 months after implementation of mild therapeutic hypothermia, had a Glasgow Coma Scale score >8, or did not satisfy Simplified Acute Physiology Score II inclusion criteria. Interventions: none. Of 13,962 admitted patients, 8,645 were excluded, including 5,544 excluded because Glasgow Coma Scale score >8. The resultant cohort included 5,317 patients: 1,547 before implementation and 3,770 after implementation. The odds ratio was adjusted for Simplified Acute Physiology Score II score, age, gender, propensity score, and in- or out-of-hospital cardiac arrest. The authors concluded that implementation of mild therapeutic hypothermia was associated with a 20% relative reduction of hospital mortality.",
  "summary": "In a Dutch retrospective observational survey of cardiac arrest patients admitted to intensive care units, implementation of mild therapeutic hypothermia was associated with lower temperatures during the first 24 hours and a 20% relative reduction in hospital mortality, with an adjusted odds ratio of 0.80 (95% CI 0.65-0.98)."
