  "other_facts": "Various agents, including chemotherapeutic drugs, can induce cell senescence; the mechanisms involved in the aging pathway, particularly chemotherapy-induced stress on telomeres, are still undefined. Human mesenchymal stem cells were assessed as target cells to investigate initiation of the aging process by chemotherapy. The mesenchymal stem cells were exposed to sublethal doses of doxorubicin 10 nM and etoposide 500 ng/mL that induce DNA double-stranded breaks. Telomere length was assessed by flow-fluorescence in situ hybridization and Southern blotting. Chemotherapy-induced telomere length shortening is a permanent signature of previous chemotherapy-mediated DNA damage and predicts impaired proliferative and differentiation potential. Cultured mesenchymal stem cells represent a useful cellular model to investigate novel drugs that may favor, or prevent, telomere length loss in human stem cells.",
