  "safety": [],
  "other_facts": "Normal human fetal development requires an adequate supply of thyroid hormone from conception. Until about 16 weeks' gestation this supply is entirely by placental transfer of maternal hormone. Subsequently, the fetal thyroid synthesizes thyroid hormones, requiring a supply of maternal iodide. Trophoblast iodide transfer is mediated by the apical sodium iodide symporter (NIS). Placental oxygen levels are low in early pregnancy (~1%), rising with placental vascularisation to a plateau of ~8% at about 16 weeks. The impact of changing oxygen levels on placental implantation is well recognized, but effects on trophoblast materno-fetal exchange are less understood. The study investigated expression of the NIS regulator hCG, NIS mRNA expression, and I(125) uptake in BeWo cells cultured in 1% and 8% oxygen and room air (21% oxygen). Desferrioxamine, an iron chelator and hypoxia mimic, decreased NIS and hCG expression and I(125) uptake. NIS expression and I(125) uptake in cells grown at 1% oxygen were not increased by addition of hCG (2,500 IU/l). The authors infer that placental NIS mRNA and protein expression are regulated by oxygen, rising with placental vascularization in the late first trimester, when fetal iodide requirements are increasing.",
  "summary": "In BeWo trophoblast-like cells, higher oxygen levels increased NIS and hCG expression and iodide uptake, indicating placental NIS expression is regulated by oxygen and may rise with placental vascularization in late first trimester."
}

