  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Fibrillary glomerulonephritis is a rare cause of progressive renal dysfunction resulting in fibrillary deposits in the mesangium and/or glomerular basement membrane. Some case reports have shown fibrillary glomerulonephritis in patients with rheumatoid arthritis and other autoimmune diseases. This is the first case report of fibrillary glomerulonephritis in a patient with Behçet's syndrome. The most common renal histological finding in Behçet's syndrome is secondary amyloidosis. Serum and urine protein electrophoresis showed no evidence of monoclonal gammopathy. A renal biopsy was performed. Light microscopy showed mesangial widening and nodular expansion with hyaline deposits. Immunofluorescence microscopy revealed immunoglobulin M deposits in the mesangium. Congo red staining was negative. Electron microscopy showed fibrillary deposits on the glomerular basement membrane. The patient had been taking azathioprine and mesalazine for 4 years due to Behçet's syndrome. No other immunosuppressive agents or corticosteroids were added. Treatment of this glomerulopathy is not promising. None of the various approaches, including corticosteroid, plasmapheresis, and cytotoxic therapy, improves prognosis.",
  "summary": "This first case report describes fibrillary glomerulonephritis in a 46-year-old woman with a 4-year history of Behçet's syndrome who presented with proteinuria and microscopic hematuria and received no additional immunosuppression beyond azathioprine and mesalazine."
}

