  "other_facts": "The physiological clearance of hyaluronic acid occurs in the liver and kidneys and depends on its molecular mass; hyaluronic acid is a mortality marker in end-stage kidney disease. The aim was to examine the effect of different modes of renal replacement therapy on low- and high-molecular-mass hyaluronic acid. Total plasma hyaluronic acid, LMWHA, and HMWHA were measured before and after HD and HDF and compared with normal controls. Plasma β2-microglobulin was determined as an independent inflammation marker. The isolated effect of the extracorporeal system on HMWHA fractions was investigated in a separate in vitro study. LMWHA and HMWHA were elevated in ESKD patients compared with healthy persons (P < 0.01). HMWHA decreased by about 40% during dialysis (P < 0.05). Plasma β2-microglobulin correlated with LMWHA (P < 0.0001) but not with total HA. In vitro dialysis runs suggested the HMWHA decrease was not caused by degradation or adsorption of HMWHA fragments. The authors concluded that the decrease in high-mass HA during HD and HDF mirrors physiological clearance initiated by HD and HDF rather than physical elimination in the extracorporeal circulation.",
