  "other_facts": "The study assessed correlates of pre- and posttransplant cardiac troponin T levels and their relationship with recipient survival. Pretransplant cardiac troponin T was a significant predictor of survival after kidney transplantation. Elevated pretransplant cardiac troponin T was defined as >=0.01 ng/mL and declined rapidly after transplantation. The association of higher pretransplant cardiac troponin T with posttransplant death/cardiac events was independent of cardiovascular risk factors. The association of elevated posttransplant cardiac troponin T with reduced survival was independent of age, diabetes, pretransplant dialysis, heart disease, and allograft function. p < 0.0001 for cardiac troponin T at 3 weeks and p < 0.0001 for cardiac troponin T at 1 year. Variables related to elevated posttransplant cardiac troponin T included pretransplant diabetes, older age, time on dialysis, high pretransplant cardiac troponin T, and lower graft function. Patients with delayed graft function and those with GFR <30 mL/min at 3 weeks were more likely to have elevated cardiac troponin T at 3 weeks and remained at high risk. When allografts restore sufficient kidney function, cardiac troponin T normalizes and patient survival improves. Lack of normalization of cardiac troponin T posttransplant identifies individuals at high risk of death/cardiac events.",
