  "other_facts": "Pregnancy-specific liver diseases such as acute fatty liver of pregnancy, hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes and low platelet syndrome, and preeclampsia-associated liver disease are associated with considerable morbidity and mortality. Hospital admission, clinical, hematologic, and biochemical data were collected from patients admitted to St. John's Medical College Hospital, Bangalore, India, from January 2000 through April 2011. Logistic regression models were fitted using the MELD score and other variables identified as clinically or statistically significant. Predictive accuracy and calibration were assessed by receiver operating characteristic curves and the Hosmer-Lemeshow goodness-of-fit test. Univariate analysis identified encephalopathy, ascites, serum total protein, bilirubin, platelet count, alkaline phosphatase, serum creatinine, and INR as significant variables. Multivariate analysis identified total bilirubin (P < .001) and INR (P < .003) as significant predictors of mortality. A model based on only bilirubin level and INR was comparable with the MELD model for predicting mortality among women with pregnancy-specific liver diseases.",
  "summary": "In a retrospective study of 130 patients with pregnancy-specific liver diseases, the MELD score and a simpler logistic model using only INR and total bilirubin accurately predicted mortality, with the 2-variable model performing comparably to MELD."
