  "other_facts": "Fibromyalgia is characterized by chronic widespread pain that leads to reduced physical function. Exercise training is commonly recommended as a treatment for management of symptoms. Resistance training is exercise performed against a progressive resistance with the intention of improving muscle strength, muscle endurance, muscle power, or a combination of these. The review evaluated benefits and harms of resistance exercise training in adults with fibromyalgia and compared resistance training versus control and versus other types of exercise training. Nine electronic databases were searched: The Cochrane Library, MEDLINE, EMBASE, CINAHL, PEDro, Dissertation Abstracts, Current Controlled Trials, World Health Organization International Clinical Trials Registry Platform, and AMED; other sources were also searched for published full-text articles. The date of the last search was 5 March 2013. Two review authors independently screened 1856 citations, 766 abstracts, and 156 full-text articles; the literature search yielded 1865 citations. Five studies met the selection criteria. Selection criteria were randomized clinical trial, diagnosis of fibromyalgia based on published criteria, adult sample, full-text publication, and inclusion of between-group data comparing resistance training versus a control or other physical activity intervention. Pairs of review authors independently assessed risk of bias and extracted intervention and outcome data. Disagreements were resolved by discussion within pairs or, when necessary, by the full team of 11 members. Twenty-one outcomes were extracted, of which seven were designated major outcomes: multidimensional function, self reported physical function, pain, tenderness, muscle strength, attrition rates, and adverse effects. Benefits and harms were evaluated using standardized mean differences, mean differences, risk ratios, or Peto odds ratios and 95% confidence intervals; meta-analysis was carried out where two or more studies provided data for an outcome. Three randomized trials compared 16 to 21 weeks of moderate- to high-intensity resistance training versus a control group. Two studies compared eight weeks of progressive resistance training using free weights or body weight resistance exercise versus aerobic training, including progressive treadmill walking and indoor and outdoor walking. One study compared 12 weeks of low-intensity resistance training using hand weights and elastic tubing versus flexibility exercise consisting of static stretches to major muscle groups. The evidence was classified low quality due to the low number of studies and risk of bias assessment. Adverse effects were generally poorly recorded, but no serious adverse effects were reported. Assessment of risk of bias was hampered by poor written descriptions, including allocation concealment and blinding of outcome assessors; lack of a priori protocols and lack of care provider blinding were methodologic concerns. Authors concluded that low-quality evidence suggested moderate- and moderate- to high-intensity resistance training improves multidimensional function, pain, tenderness, and muscle strength in women with fibromyalgia; eight weeks of aerobic exercise was superior to moderate-intensity resistance training for improving pain; 12 weeks of low-intensity resistance training was superior to flexibility exercise training for improvements in pain and multidimensional function; and women with fibromyalgia can safely perform moderate- to high-resistance training.",
  "summary": "This low-quality systematic review and meta-analysis of five randomized clinical trials found that resistance training improves multidimensional function, pain, tenderness, and muscle strength in women with fibromyalgia, with no serious adverse effects reported."
