  "other_facts": "In x-ray mammography, flattening of the breast improves image quality and reduces absorbed dose. Current compression guidelines apply standardized force without considering breast size, causing large variations in pressure. Hypothesis: pressure-controlled compression taking contact area into account improves standardization of physiological conditions in the compressed breast (blood pressure), reduces discomfort and pain, especially severe pain complaints, with limited effects on image quality and absorbed glandular dose. Pain scores before and after compression were obtained using an 11-point numerical rating scale; scores >=7 indicated severe pain. Breast thickness, applied force, contact area between breast and compression paddle, and mean pressure were recorded during the entire compression. Differences between craniocaudal and mediolateral oblique compressions, correlation coefficients, and odds ratios for predictors of severe pain were analyzed using multivariate logistic regression. Observed data were used in two models to estimate breast thickness, required force, and pain score for pressure-controlled compression protocols with target pressures ranging from 4 to 28 kPa. A selection of 79 mammograms with a 10% or more thickness difference relative to the prior mammogram was used in a retrospective observer study. A standardized pressure of 10 kPa corresponds with normal arterial blood pressure.",
