    "key_characteristics": "18 patients with idiopathic intracranial hypertension and transverse sinus stenosis with a mean pressure gradient of at least 4 mmHg underwent venous sinus stenting; 3 patients required shunting after stent placement and 15 patients did not need further treatment; shunting-after-stent group mean body mass index was 36.6 kg/m^2; stent-alone group mean body mass index was 33.3 kg/m^2; pre-intervention opening CSF pressure was 50 cm H2O in the shunting-after-stent group and 37 cm H2O in the stent-alone group."
  },
  "intervention": {
    "name": "venous sinus stenting",
    "dose": null,
    "frequency": null,
    "duration": null
  },
  "outcomes": [
    {
      "measure": "mean opening CSF pressure",
      "timepoint": "most recent lumbar puncture prior to any intervention",
      "effect": "50 cm H2O in the shunting-after-stent group vs 37 cm H2O in the stent-alone group",
      "ci": null
    },
    {
      "measure": "need for further treatment after venous sinus stenting",
      "timepoint": "after venous sinus stenting",
      "effect": "15/18 did not need further treatment; 3/18 required shunt placement",
      "ci": null
    }
  ],
  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension, when no underlying etiology is found, is a clinical syndrome characterized by elevated intracranial pressure (>25 cm H2O), which may lead to headaches and visual symptoms. In patients with idiopathic intracranial hypertension and transverse sinus stenosis, placement of a venous stent across the stenosis has been shown in several case series to lower intracranial pressure and resolve symptoms, with generally favorable results. The study examined common risk factors associated with failure of transvenous stenting for idiopathic intracranial hypertension. If venous sinus stenting fails, CSF diversion should be considered as the next line of treatment. Pre- and post-treatment neurological and neuro-ophthalmological evaluations were compared between the 15 patients who did not need further treatment and the 3 patients who underwent shunt placement as second-line treatment. The difference in pre-intervention opening CSF pressure between groups was statistically significant (p<0.05). There were no other significant differences in pre- or post-intervention factors between the two groups. In patients with idiopathic intracranial hypertension and documented venous sinus stenosis with a pressure gradient, venous sinus stenting should be the primary treatment of choice; however, some patients may be refractory to stenting and still require permanent CSF diversion, which can be complicated in chronically anticoagulated patients. Patients with persistent papilledema post-stenting and highly elevated opening pressure pre-stenting should be followed closely because they are at greatest risk of requiring a shunt and failing stenting.",
  "summary": "In a retrospective series of 18 patients with idiopathic intracranial hypertension and transverse sinus stenosis treated with venous sinus stenting, higher pre-stenting opening pressure and persistent papilledema identified patients at greater risk of stenting failure requiring CSF diversion."
}

