  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Aortic stiffness assessed by carotid-to-femoral PWV often fails to predict cardiovascular risk and mortality in the very elderly; this may be due to the non-linear association between PWV and compliance or to blood pressure decrease in the frailest subjects. Total arterial compliance is the most relevant arterial property regarding cardiovascular function compared with local or regional arterial stiffness. A new method for total arterial compliance estimation based on PWV was recently proposed. The study aimed to investigate the value of C_T to predict all-cause mortality in the elderly. Total arterial compliance was estimated by the formula C_T = k x PWV^-2. Coefficient k is body-size dependent based on previous in silico simulations. k was adjusted for body mass index, with a 10% change in BMI corresponding to almost 11% change in k. For reference BMI 26.2 kg/m^2, k=37. The association of C_T with mortality was assessed after adjustment for gender, mean pressure, and heart rate. Age was an independent determinant of C_T (p=0.016) but not of PWV. C_T estimated by a novel method can predict all-cause mortality in the elderly. C_T may be a more sensitive arterial biomarker than PWV regarding cardiovascular risk assessment.",
  "summary": "In 279 elderly subjects followed for a mean 12.8 months, total arterial compliance estimated from pulse wave velocity predicted all-cause mortality, whereas pulse wave velocity did not."
}

