  "other_facts": "Renal sympathetic denervation is a treatment option for resistant arterial hypertension but is unsuccessful in some patients; predictive parameters were previously unknown. The study evaluated whether soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase-1 (sFLT-1), intercellular cell adhesion molecule-1 (ICAM-1), and vascular cell adhesion molecule-1 (VCAM-1) are predictive markers for blood pressure reduction after RSD. sFLT-1, ICAM-1, and VCAM-1 are associated with endothelial dysfunction, vascular remodeling, and hypertension. Venous serum samples were collected before and 6 months after RSD. Therapeutic response was defined as an office systolic blood pressure reduction of >10 mm Hg 6 months after RSD. At baseline, levels were significantly higher in responders than nonresponders for sFLT-1 (P<0.001), ICAM-1 (P<0.001), and VCAM-1 (P<0.01). For the predictive areas under the curve, the interquartile ranges and P values were: sFLT-1 0.718-0.921 (P<0.001), ICAM-1 0.654-0.854 (P<0.001), and VCAM-1 0.564-804 (P=0.01). The study identified for the first time potential biomarkers with predictive value for responder or nonresponder status before renal denervation.",
