  "other_facts": "Purpose: to determine predictors and consequences of hemodynamic instability after carotid artery stenting. Records of all patients undergoing carotid artery stenting in a single institution were reviewed; demographics, risk factors, indications, medications including statins, atropine, and beta blockers, anatomic risk factors, balloon and stent length and diameter, and degree of stenosis were noted. Periprocedural hypertension was defined as systolic blood pressure >160, hypotension as systolic blood pressure <90, and bradycardia as heart rate <60, lasting longer than 1 hour; more transient hemodynamic instability was also documented. Rates of transient ischemic attack, stroke, myocardial infarction, and death within 30 days were calculated. Chi-squared analysis was used to determine predictors of hemodynamic instability and whether hemodynamic instability was associated with major adverse events. A total of 199 carotid artery stenting procedures were performed. Octogenarians were more likely to experience both transient and prolonged hemodynamic instability; angina or contralateral occlusion predicted any hemodynamic instability; female sex predicted prolonged hemodynamic instability. Periprocedural hemodynamic instability occurs frequently even with prophylactic atropine administration.",
