  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Treatment of suspected infection is a mainstay of daily NICU work. The study hypothesized that NICU antibiotic prescribing practice variation correlates with rates of proven infection, necrotizing enterocolitis, mortality, inborn admission, NICU surgical volume, and average length of stay. The study compared sample means and explored linear and nonparametric correlations. Overall antibiotic use varied 40-fold, from 2.4% to 97.1% of patient-days; median antibiotic use was 24.5% of patient-days. Fifty percent of intermediate level NICUs were in the highest antibiotic use quartile, yet most of these units reported infection rates of zero. The authors concluded that 40-fold variation in antibiotic prescribing across 127 NICUs with similar burdens of proven infection, necrotizing enterocolitis, surgical volume, and mortality indicates that a considerable portion of antibiotic use lacks clear warrant; in some NICUs, antibiotics are overused. Additional study is needed to establish appropriate use ranges and elucidate the determinants and directionality of relationships between antibiotic and other resource use.",
