  "other_facts": "The liver has a central role in glucose homeostasis and is an important target for drug development for type 2 diabetes mellitus; significant species differences in liver metabolism necessitate supplementation of animal data with human-relevant assays; isolated primary human hepatocytes display rapid decline in phenotypic functions in conventional monolayer formats; cocultivation of primary human hepatocytes with specific stromal cells, especially in micropatterned configurations, can stabilize some liver functions for ~4 weeks in vitro; it was previously unclear whether coculture approaches can stabilize hormone-modulated glucose metabolism in primary human hepatocytes; this study compared commonly employed conventional culture formats and previously developed micropatterned cocultures of cryopreserved primary human hepatocytes and stromal fibroblasts; micropatterned cocultures provide a platform to study glucose metabolism and hormonal responsiveness in cryopreserved primary human hepatocytes from multiple donors for several weeks in vitro; the model is useful to study effects of drugs and overnutrition for type 2 diabetes mellitus; proof-of-concept experiments tested gluconeogenesis inhibitors and high-glucose stimulation as in type 2 diabetes mellitus.",
  "summary": "Micropatterned cocultures of cryopreserved primary human hepatocytes and stromal fibroblasts maintained glucose-metabolism gene expression, hormone-responsive gluconeogenesis, glycogen dynamics, metformin response, and high-glucose-induced lipid accumulation for weeks, providing an in vitro platform for type 2 diabetes mellitus research."
