  "other_facts": "Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD) is a severe lung disease of preterm infants characterized by fewer, enlarged alveoli and increased inflammation; BPD has grave consequences and no effective and safe therapy exists. The authors previously showed that prophylactic interleukin-1 receptor antagonist (IL-1Ra) prevents murine BPD induced by perinatal inflammation and hyperoxia. The current study used the same BPD model to assess whether the alternative anti-inflammatory agent protein C (PC) is as effective as IL-1Ra against BPD, tested delayed administration and a higher dose of IL-1Ra, and investigated aspects of drug safety. Study arms included vehicle, 1200 IU/kg PC, 10 mg/kg IL-1Ra with early or late onset, and 100 mg/kg IL-1Ra given as daily injections while pups were reared in 21%, 65%, or 85% O2. After 3 or 28 days, lung and brain histology were assessed and pulmonary cytokines were analyzed using ELISA and cytokine arrays. PC only moderately reduced the severe impact of BPD on lung structure. Low-dose IL-1Ra was more effective than high-dose IL-1Ra only when treatment commenced at day 1 of life. The authors concluded that prophylactic low-dose IL-1Ra and PC ameliorate BPD and have potential as the first remedy for one of the most devastating diseases preterm babies face.",
