  "safety": [],
  "other_facts": "Gene expression was evaluated in histologically normal-appearing tissue adjacent to prostate tumor in radical prostatectomy specimens to assess biological significance based on prediction of clinical recurrence, defined as metastatic disease or local recurrence. Expression of 732 genes was measured by quantitative reverse transcriptase polymerase chain reaction separately in tumor and adjacent normal-appearing tissue specimens from 127 patients with and 374 without clinical recurrence following radical prostatectomy for T1/T2 prostate cancer. A total of 410 evaluable patients had paired tumor and normal-appearing tissue. Forty-six genes representing diverse biological pathways (androgen signaling, stromal response, stress response, cellular organization, proliferation, cell adhesion, and chromatin remodeling) were associated with clinical recurrence in normal-appearing tissue (FDR < 20%), of which 39 concordantly predicted clinical recurrence in tumor (FDR < 20%). A 17-gene expression signature, the Genomic Prostate Score, previously validated to predict aggressive prostate cancer when measured in tumor tissue, was assessed using pre-specified genes and algorithms. Analysis used Cox proportional hazards models, Storey's false discovery rate control, and regression to the mean correction. The authors concluded that gene expression profiles, including Genomic Prostate Score, from normal-appearing tissue adjacent to tumor can predict prostate cancer outcome and suggest a biologically significant field effect in primary prostate cancer that is a marker for aggressive disease.",
  "summary": "In 410 patients with T1/T2 prostate cancer after radical prostatectomy, gene expression profiles in histologically normal-appearing tissue adjacent to tumor, especially the Genomic Prostate Score, predicted clinical recurrence and suggested a biologically significant field effect."
}

