  "other_facts": "Emerging evidence suggests glycemic variability may be a more reliable measure of glycemic control than mean HbA1c in type 2 diabetes mellitus. The study aimed to determine if HbA1c variability is associated with cardiovascular events in type 2 diabetic patients and if different renal functions affect this association. Patients were enrolled from the Kaohsiung Medical University Research Database in 2009 and followed until 2015. Intra-individual HbA1c variability was defined as the standard deviation of HbA1c. Patients were grouped by eGFR >=60 or <60 mL/min/1.73m2. In an adjusted Cox model, high HbA1c standard deviation was associated with significantly increased cardiovascular event risk in patients with eGFR >=60 mL/min/1.73m2, but mean HbA1c was not associated; the association was not seen in patients with eGFR <60 mL/min/1.73m2. The study concluded that greater HbA1c variability is associated with increased cardiovascular risk among patients with preserved renal function, but not in those with moderate to advanced chronic kidney disease.",
