  "other_facts": "Cancer cells have a proliferative advantage by utilizing intermediates of aerobic glycolysis (Warburg effect) for macromolecule synthesis. The exact causes of the Warburg effect are unclear, but high osmotic stress in the solid tumor microenvironment is considered an important factor. Oleanolic acid is known to exert anti-inflammatory and anti-cancer effects. Hypertonic culture conditions were 0.16 M NaCl. The 26% increase in cancer cell growth, 34% increase in glucose consumption, and 25% increase in lactate production were observed over untreated cells and were statistically significant (p < 0.05 for each). Enhanced aerobic glycolysis in cancer cells is associated with decreased mitochondrial activity and mitochondrial-associated caspase activity. OA efficiently reversed high salt-mediated enhanced expression of aerobic glycolytic enzymes and decreased cancer cell proliferation. High salt further inhibited mitochondrial-related cytochrome oxidase and caspase-3 activity. OA efficiently reversed high salt-mediated inhibition of cytochrome oxidase, caspase activity, and pro-apoptotic Bax expression, suggesting that OA induced mitochondrial activity and enhanced apoptosis. The data indicate that OA efficiently reverses enhanced Warburg-like metabolism induced by high salt-mediated osmotic stress and supports potential application of OA in anti-cancer therapy.",
