  "other_facts": "Air pollution exposure has been reported to influence blood pressure; few epidemiological studies have demonstrated an association between ambient air pollution exposure and acute hypertensive events. The study examined the association between gaseous air pollutants and hospital admissions for hypertension in Taipei, Taiwan using data from 2009-2013. Odds ratios were calculated for each interquartile range increase in each gaseous air pollutant using a case-crossover approach, after controlling for weather variables, day of the week, seasonality, and long-term time trends. In the single-pollutant model, significant correlation was observed between hospital admissions for hypertension and ozone levels on both warm (>23°C) and cool (<23°C) days. No significant associations were found between levels of other gaseous pollutants and risk of hospital admissions for hypertension. In the two-pollutant model, ozone levels remained significant after inclusion of particulate matter 10 μm or less in diameter (PM10), sulfur dioxide (SO2), or carbon monoxide (CO) on warm days. On cool days, ozone levels remained significant after inclusion of air pollutants other than SO2. The findings indicate that ozone exposure may trigger a rise in blood pressure to a level that results in higher numbers of hospital admissions.",
