  "other_facts": "GTCS, syncope, and PNES are common emergent neurological conditions causing transient disturbances of consciousness and can be difficult to distinguish. A total of 391 patients were retrospectively analyzed. Group sizes were GTCS n=179, syncope n=156, and PNES n=56. Venous blood was drawn within 20 min of emergency department arrival, and serum uric acid was measured using the uricase method. Serum uric acid level and elevation percentage were significantly higher in GTCS than syncope and PNES (both p<0.001); separate male and female GTCS comparisons remained significant (all p<0.001). Within GTCS, males had higher serum uric acid (p<0.001) and higher elevation percentage (p<0.05) than females. Time to normalization was analyzed in 40 GTCS patients: 23 males and 17 females; duration did not differ significantly by sex (p=0.325). The authors concluded serum uric acid has clinical value for differentiating GTCS, syncope, and PNES but requires verification in prospective studies with larger sample sizes.",
