  "other_facts": "Observational evidence suggests higher nut consumption is associated with better glycemic control, but it is unclear whether this association is causal. A total of 1063 potentially eligible articles were screened in duplicate; 40 articles were eligible for inclusion, and data were extracted in duplicate. The meta-analysis included 40 randomized controlled trials. Weighted mean differences between nut intervention and control arms were determined using the DerSimonian and Laird random-effects method. For outcomes where a limited number of studies were published, a qualitative synthesis was presented. Heterogeneity was I2 = 51.7% for HOMA-IR, I2 = 49.4% for fasting insulin, I2 = 53.4% for fasting blood glucose, and I2 = 51.0% for HbA1c. Consumption of peanuts or tree nuts significantly decreased HOMA-IR and fasting insulin, with no effect on HbA1c or fasting glucose. The results suggest nut consumption may improve insulin sensitivity. Well-designed clinical trials are required to elucidate mechanisms.",
