  "other_facts": "Diabetic peripheral neuropathy is one of the most severe complications of diabetes mellitus. Limited evidence was available on the prevalence of diabetic peripheral neuropathy among patients with diabetes mellitus in Ethiopia, and there were no updated studies estimating the national prevalence of diabetic peripheral neuropathy in Ethiopia before this review. The study was submitted for registration with PROSPERO in March 2020 and accepted with registration number CRD42020173831. Databases searched included PubMed, Scopus, Google Scholar, African Journals Online, WHO Afro Library, and Cochrane Review. The reviewers used the PRISMA guideline. All published and unpublished articles were analyzed. A random effects model was used to estimate pooled prevalence. Statistical analysis used R version 3.5.3 and RStudio version 1.2.5033 for Windows. Heterogeneity was evaluated by the inconsistency index I2. Publication bias was examined by funnel plot. The database search produced 245 papers. After inclusion and exclusion criteria, 38 articles with 14029 total patients with diabetes mellitus were included. Except for three retrospective cohort studies, all included studies were cross-sectional. The authors concluded that more than one-fifth of patients with diabetes have diabetic peripheral neuropathy, that the prevalence in Ethiopia is considerably high, and that attention should be given to monitoring patients' blood glucose.",
  "summary": "This systematic review and meta-analysis of 38 studies including 14,029 patients with diabetes mellitus in Ethiopia found a pooled diabetic peripheral neuropathy prevalence of 22% (95% CI 18% to 26%), with regional subgroup estimates of 23% in Addis Ababa, 27% in Oromia, 16% in South nation and nationalities, and 15% in Amhara."
}

