  "other_facts": "Disruption of the intestinal barrier is a key component of various diseases; whether barrier dysfunction is cause or effect is still unknown; translocation of luminal content may initiate gastrointestinal or systemic inflammatory disorders; trauma- or infection-driven epithelial permeability depends on Toll-like receptor activity; inhibition of TLR signaling has been proposed as a strategy to protect intestinal barrier integrity after infection or other pathological conditions; selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors including sertraline and citalopram were shown to inhibit TLR-3 activity; the direct effects of these antidepressant drugs on the gut mucosa barrier were largely unexplored; two approaches were used: ex vivo studies in isolated intestinal tissue and in vivo testing in a rat model of disrupted gut barrier induced by a low protein diet; both compounds were tested for preventive effects; only sertraline increased transepithelial electrical resistance in rat colon ex vivo at 0.8 μg/mL over 180 min and in vivo at 30 mg/kg p.o. over 20 days; citalopram at 20 mg/kg p.o. over 20 days prevented the increase in phospho-IRF3 protein in low-protein diet rat ileum, but sertraline did not; neither antidepressant affected locomotion, anxiety-like behaviours, or stress-induced defecation; the authors concluded that the data support investigation of sertraline as a therapeutic strategy to protect intestinal barrier function under life-threatening situations or chronic conditions associated with gut epithelial disruption.",
