{
  "study": {
    "design": "systematic review and meta-analysis of crossover experimental studies",
    "year": "1978-2020",
    "sample_size": "19 studies",
    "follow_up": null
  },
  "population": {
    "age": null,
    "age_criteria": null,
    "sex": null,
    "weight": null,
    "key_characteristics": "individuals performing submaximal aerobic exercise after a fasting period; fitness levels included sedentary or untrained individuals and trained or recreational athletes"
  },
  "intervention": {
    "name": "caffeine",
    "dose": "2-7 mg/kg of body mass",
    "frequency": "acute pre-exercise intake",
    "duration": "acute"
  },
  "outcomes": [
    {
      "measure": "fat oxidation rate",
      "timepoint": "during exercise after fasting",
      "effect": "SMD = 0.73",
      "ci": "95% CI = 0.19 to 1.27"
    },
    {
      "measure": "respiratory exchange ratio",
      "timepoint": "during exercise after fasting",
      "effect": "SMD = -0.33",
      "ci": "95% CI = -0.65 to -0.01"
    },
    {
      "measure": "oxygen uptake",
      "timepoint": "during exercise after fasting",
      "effect": "SMD = 0.23",
      "ci": "95% CI = 0.01 to 0.44"
    }
  ],
  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Previous investigations have been designed to determine the effect of acute caffeine intake on the rate of fat oxidation during exercise; however, they showed contradictory results due to differences in exercise protocols used or co-ingestion of caffeine with other substances, and there is no consensus. The study conducted a systematic review followed by a meta-analysis to establish the effect of acute caffeine intake ranging from 2 to 7 mg/kg of body mass on the rate of fat oxidation during exercise. A total of 19 studies published between 1978 and 2020 were included. All included studies employed crossover experimental designs in which ingestion of caffeine was compared to placebo. Studies were selected if exercise intensity was consistent in caffeine and placebo trials and if these trials were preceded by a fasting protocol. Meta-analysis was performed using the random effects model to calculate the standardized mean difference. Caffeine significantly increased fat oxidation rate (p = 0.008), significantly reduced respiratory exchange ratio (p = 0.04), and significantly increased oxygen uptake (p = 0.049). There was a dose-response effect of caffeine on fat oxidation rate, indicating that more than 3.0 mg/kg is necessary to obtain a statistically significant effect. The ability of caffeine to enhance fat oxidation during exercise was higher in sedentary or untrained individuals than in trained and recreational athletes. Pre-exercise intake of a moderate dose of caffeine may effectively increase fat utilization during aerobic exercise of submaximal intensity performed after a fasting period. Fitness level may modulate the magnitude of the effect of caffeine on fat oxidation during exercise.",
  "summary": "This systematic review and meta-analysis of 19 crossover studies found that acute pre-exercise caffeine intake significantly increases fat oxidation during fasting submaximal aerobic exercise, with effects modulated by dose and fitness level."
}

