  "other_facts": "Finerenone is a nonsteroidal selective mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist. It recently demonstrated efficacy in delaying chronic kidney disease progression and reducing cardiovascular events in patients with chronic kidney disease and type 2 diabetes in FIDELIO-DKD. In FIDELIO-DKD, 5734 patients were randomized 1:1 to receive either titrated finerenone doses of 10 or 20 mg once daily or placebo. Nonlinear mixed-effects population pharmacokinetic models were used to analyze pharmacokinetics, with sparse sampling in all subjects receiving finerenone. Post-hoc model parameter estimates together with dosing histories allowed computation of individual exposures used in parametric time-to-event analyses of the primary kidney outcome. The population pharmacokinetic model adequately captured the typical pharmacokinetics of finerenone and its variability. Low estimated glomerular filtration rate and high urine-to-creatinine ratio increased the risk of treatment-independent chronic kidney disease progression, while concomitant sodium-glucose transport protein 2 inhibitor use decreased the risk. No sodium-glucose transport protein 2 inhibitor co-medication-related modification of the finerenone treatment effect per se could be identified. None of the tested pharmacokinetic covariates had clinical relevance. Finerenone effects on kidney outcomes approached saturation towards 20 mg once daily, and sodium-glucose transport protein 2 inhibitor use provided additive benefits.",
  "summary": "Population pharmacokinetic and exposure-response analyses from FIDELIO-DKD showed that finerenone exposure was not meaningfully affected by tested covariates, kidney-outcome benefit approached saturation at 20 mg once daily, and SGLT2 inhibitor use provided additive prognostic benefit."
}

