  "other_facts": "In rheumatoid arthritis, macrophages modulate the immunoinflammatory response through polarization into classically activated M1 or alternatively activated M2 phenotypes. CTLA4-Ig, abatacept, reduces macrophage inflammatory activity by interacting with the costimulatory molecule CD86. The study aimed to investigate whether CTLA4-Ig induces an M2 phenotype in M1-polarised monocyte-derived macrophages from healthy subjects and in cultured monocyte-derived macrophages from active rheumatoid arthritis patients. Monocyte-derived macrophages were obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells after stimulation with phorbol myristate acetate 5 ng/mL for 24 h. Healthy-subject macrophages were stimulated with lipopolysaccharide 1 mg/mL for 4 h to induce M1 macrophages. M1-polarised healthy-subject macrophages and rheumatoid arthritis macrophages were treated with CTLA4-Ig 100 μM and 500 μM for 3, 12, 24, and 48 h. Gene expression of CD80, CD86, TLR4, CD163, CD204, CD206, and MerTK was evaluated by qRT-PCR. Protein synthesis of surface M2 markers was investigated by Western blotting. Statistical analysis was performed by the Wilcoxon t-test. The authors concluded that CTLA4-Ig seems to induce an in vitro shift from M1 to M2 macrophages in both healthy-subject M1 macrophages and rheumatoid arthritis macrophages, suggesting an additional mechanism for modulation of the rheumatoid arthritis inflammatory process.",
