  "other_facts": "HDFN is a clinically significant problem that may potentially affect any pregnancy, and DAT is considered an important test for identifying newborns suspected to have HDN. The study reviewed data over 10 years regarding positive DAT etiology and development of HDN. Data were obtained from patients' electronic hospital files, transfusion medicine databases, and medical birth records, and laboratory parameters and clinical interventions were compared between DAT-positive and DAT-negative neonates. Among DAT-positive cases, ABO-incompatibility was the most common cause (59.1%), Rh incompatibility accounted for 13.8%, minor blood group incompatibility and other RBC-related antibodies 10.1%, and unspecified etiology 17%; ABO-incompatibility was also the major reason for HDN. Rare but important conditions may lead to a positive DAT result, such as antibodies passively acquired from mothers in the context of alloimmunizations or using drugs. The study concluded that there is a crucial need for increasing awareness regarding early diagnosis, careful monitoring, and employment of prenatal alloimmunization screening tests.",
