  "other_facts": "Hyperhemolysis syndrome is a severe delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction seen in sickle cell disease patients, characterized by destruction of donor and recipient RBCs; it results in a drop in hemoglobin to below pretransfusion levels and frequently reticulocytopenia. The patient was hospitalized 2 weeks prior for COVID-19. At that time, his red cell antibody history included anti-Fy(a) and warm autoantibody; he was given 2 units of RBC and discharged with hemoglobin 10.2 g/dl. He returned approximately 1.5 weeks later with hemoglobin 6.0 g/dl and symptoms concerning for acute chest syndrome. Pretransfusion testing showed 4+ pan-agglutinin in both gel-based and tube-based testing. Alloadsorption identified anti-N and a strong cold agglutinin. Three least incompatible units were transfused over several days, with evidence of hemolysis. Further reference lab work revealed anti-Fya, anti-Fyb, anti-Lea, anti-Leb, and an anti-KN system antibody. The hemoglobin nadir was 4.4 g/dl. The patient was treated with a single dose of tocilizumab, his hemoglobin stabilized, and he was discharged. Information on the relationship between SARS-CoV-2 infection and hyperhemolysis syndrome is limited; inflammation related to COVID-19 could predispose to hyperhemolysis syndrome. Tocilizumab is an approved treatment for COVID-19 and appears to be a promising treatment option for hyperhemolysis syndrome.",
