  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Neisseria gonorrhoeae has developed resistance to all first-line recommended therapies, making gonococcal antimicrobial resistance a major public health concern given limited antibiotic options and an even smaller antimicrobial development pipeline. Since the CDC 2015 STD Treatment Guidelines, azithromycin, part of the 2015 dual-drug treatment regimen, has had a rapid rise in resistance. The 2020 CDC Gonorrhea Treatment Recommendations and the 2021 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines were developed weighing the priorities of treating the individual, protecting the population, and preventing antimicrobial resistance. Gonorrhea subject matter experts generated 8 key questions and conducted a literature review of updated data from 2013 to 2019 on gonorrhea antimicrobial resistance, treatment failures, clinical trials, and other key topics. More than 2200 abstracts were assessed, and 248 clinically relevant articles were thoroughly reviewed. Subject matter experts also evaluated Neisseria gonorrhoeae antimicrobial resistance data from the Gonococcal Isolate Surveillance Project. New pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic data are available, and there is greater recognition of the need for antimicrobial stewardship. The 2021 CDC STI Treatment Guidelines recommend 500 mg ceftriaxone intramuscularly once for uncomplicated gonorrhea at all anatomic sites. If coinfection with chlamydia has not been excluded, cotreatment with doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days should be added. Few alternative therapies exist for persons with cephalosporin allergies; there are no recommended alternative therapies for Neisseria gonorrhoeae infection of the throat.",
  "summary": "This 2021 CDC guideline-supporting literature review found stable US ceftriaxone susceptibility but rising azithromycin resistance and recommends single-dose 500 mg intramuscular ceftriaxone for uncomplicated gonorrhea, with doxycycline cotreatment if chlamydia has not been excluded."
