  "other_facts": "Cerebrovascular compliance contributes to dynamic regulation of cerebral blood flow, but mechanisms regulating cerebrovascular compliance in humans are unknown. Vascular compliance index (Ci) was assessed using a Windkessel model applied to blood pressure waveforms obtained by finger photoplethysmography and corresponding middle cerebral artery blood velocity or brachial artery blood velocity waveforms obtained by Doppler ultrasound. Data were analyzed during a 5-minute baseline period (10 waveforms) under control conditions and during distinct sympathetic blockade (experiment 1, phentolamine; 10 adults), cholinergic blockade (experiment 2, glycopyrrolate; 9 adults), and myogenic blockade (experiment 3, nicardipine; 14 adults). Phentolamine increased Ci similarly in the cerebral and forearm vascular beds (P = 0.45). Glycopyrrolate increased cerebrovascular and forearm Ci to a similar extent (P = 0.88). Nicardipine increased Ci to a greater extent in the cerebral vascular bed than the forearm vascular bed (P = 0.01). Adrenergic, cholinergic, and myogenic mechanisms contribute to regulation of cerebrovascular and forearm vascular compliance; myogenic mechanisms appear to exert more specific control over vascular compliance in the brain relative to the forearm. Vascular compliance is an important determinant of the dynamics and regulation of blood flow through a vascular bed, and mechanisms regulating vascular compliance remain poorly understood.",
  "summary": "This retrospective human study found that adrenergic, cholinergic, and myogenic mechanisms regulate cerebrovascular and forearm vascular compliance, with myogenic mechanisms showing greater cerebrovascular-specific control."
}

