  "other_facts": "Clinicians and laboratories routinely use urinalysis parameters to determine whether antimicrobial treatment and/or urine cultures are needed. The performance of individual urinalysis parameters and common thresholds for action are not well defined and may vary across different patient populations. Urinalyses were ordered 24 hours prior to urine culture. The study included all encounters at 3 North Carolina hospitals between 2015 and 2020 during a 6-year study period. Significant bacteriuria was defined as ≥100,000 colony-forming units of bacteria/mL. The investigators evaluated relevant urinalysis parameters as potential outcome predictors, including sensitivity, specificity, negative predictive value, and positive predictive value. They combined 18 different urinalysis criteria and used receiver operating curves to identify the 5 best-performing models for predicting significant bacteriuria. When used as part of a diagnostic workup, urinalysis parameters should be leveraged for their negative predictive value instead of sensitivity. Because many laboratories and hospitals use reflex urine culture algorithms, their workflow should include clinical decision support and/or education to target symptomatic patients and focus on populations where absence of pyuria has high negative predictive value.",
