  "other_facts": "Cardiovascular diseases are the leading cause of morbidity and mortality in Indonesia, and the CVD situation has not been well-documented. Identifying individuals at high risk of CVDs is important for Indonesian health planners in designing effective intervention strategies. The study aimed to estimate the distribution of estimated 10-year CVD risk and associated socio-demographic factors and healthcare access. Data were collected using interviews and physical measurements in 390 villages from January 2020 to February 2024. Estimated 10-year CVD risk was calculated using WHO/International Society of Hypertension region-specific charts for the Southeast Asia Region (SEAR B). Multilevel logistic regression modelling examined associations between individual and healthcare provider densities and estimated 10-year CVD risk, as well as receiving optimal preventive treatment. The urban-rural difference in high CVD risk was statistically significant (p<0.001). High-risk males living in urban areas showed better treatment. Availability of community-based health care (Posbindu), medical doctor at primary healthcare, nurses, and health insurance were associated with lower odds of high CVD risk. Around one-fifth of the population aged 40+ in Malang District had high 10-year CVD risk, and three-quarters of high-risk individuals did not receive optimal preventive treatment; accessibility of preventive care in primary care is important to address sex and geographical gaps, especially in low- and middle-income countries.",
