  "other_facts": "Renal dysfunction can lead to insulin resistance and increase the incidence of type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM). The blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio (NCR) is a frequently used indicator to assess renal dysfunction and differentiate between prerenal and intrinsic renal injury. The association between NCR and T2DM in the Chinese population was previously unclear. The relationship between NCR and T2DM was examined using Cox proportional hazards model and curve fitting techniques. Sensitivity and subgroup analyses were performed. All results were presented as hazard ratios (HRs) and 95% confidence intervals (CIs). NCR quartiles were Q1 ≤13.536, Q2 13.536-16.256, Q3 16.256-19.638, and Q4 >19.638. The fully adjusted continuous NCR association had P < .001. The higher NCR group comparison (≥20 vs <20) had P < .001. Findings were validated using sensitivity and subgroup analyses. The study concluded that there was a positive and independent association between NCR and the incidence of T2DM after adjusting for confounding variables.",
