  "other_facts": "Acute kidney injury is common after major abdominal surgery; selection of candidate kidney protective strategies for testing in large trials should be based on robust preliminary evidence. The Restrictive versus Liberal Fluid Therapy in Major Abdominal Surgery (RELIEF) trial randomly assigned adult patients undergoing major abdominal surgery to a restrictive or liberal perioperative fluid regimen. Two multivariable ordinal regression models tested the primary hypothesis that modifiable risk factors associated with increased maximum stage of postoperative acute kidney injury could be identified; each model used a separate variable-selection approach to assess sensitivity to modeling approach. Model 1 variable selection was informed by investigator opinion; model 2 used the Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator (LASSO) technique to develop a data-driven model from available variables. The model 1 findings were unchanged in model 2. Avoiding intraoperative nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs or cyclooxygenase-2 inhibitors is a potential strategy to mitigate postoperative acute kidney injury risk, and the findings strengthen the rationale for a clinical trial comprehensively testing the risk-benefit ratio of these drugs in the perioperative period.",
  "summary": "In a secondary analysis of 2,444 adults undergoing major abdominal surgery in the RELIEF trial, intraoperative NSAID/COX-2 inhibitor use and day-of-surgery ACE inhibitor/ARB use were associated with increased odds of greater maximum postoperative acute kidney injury stage, and postoperative day-1 nadir hemoglobin was inversely associated with greater maximum acute kidney injury stage."
}

