  "other_facts": "The study aimed to assess the combined effects of blood pressure and glucose status on chronic kidney disease incidence in young and middle-aged adults. An interval-censored Cox proportional hazards model with covariates was used. Hazard ratios were per 1-standard deviation, that is, 15 mmHg increase in systolic blood pressure. In participants without antihypertensive treatment, the adjusted hazard ratios were significantly higher in the borderline glycemia and diabetes groups compared with the normoglycemia group. The interaction between blood pressure and borderline glycemia was evident when the outcome definition was restricted to proteinuria. In participants under antihypertensive treatment, systolic blood pressure was most strongly associated with CKD risk in the diabetes group, although no significant interaction was observed. High blood pressure and high glucose status may synergistically increase the incidence of CKD. Strict blood pressure management may play an important role in the early prevention of CKD in individuals with worse glucose status within the young and middle-aged population. This large-scale longitudinal cohort study showed high blood pressure and diabetes synergistically increased the risk of CKD in individuals without antihypertensive treatment.",
