      "timepoint": "3 years",
      "effect": "0.787",
      "ci": null
    }
  ],
  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Subjects were diagnosed with new-onset prediabetes at the Physical Examination Center of the First Affiliated Hospital of Soochow University from October 1, 2015 to May 31, 2023 and completed 3-year follow-up. After 3 years, subjects were divided into a diabetes group and a non-diabetes group and baseline data were compared. A prediction model was established using logistic regression, a nomogram was drawn, and calibration was depicted. Logistic regression showed ageing, elevated BMI, male gender, high fasting blood glucose, increased LDL-C, fatty liver, and liver dysfunction were risk factors for progression from PreDM to diabetes within 3 years (P<0.05), while HDL-C was a protective factor (P<0.05). Derived formula: In(p/1-p)=0.181×age (40-54 years old)/0.973×age (55-74 years old)/1.868×age (≥75 years old)-0.192×gender (male)+0.151×blood glucose-0.538×BMI (24-28)-0.538×BMI (≥28)-0.109×HDL-C+0.021×LDL-C+0.365×fatty liver (yes)+0.444×liver dysfunction (yes)-10.038. The model was based on 8 influencing factors: age, BMI, gender, fasting blood glucose, LDL-C, HDL-C, fatty liver, and liver dysfunction. The AUC was 0.787, indicating good predictive ability, and the model showed good discrimination and calibration.",
  "summary": "In patients with newly diagnosed prediabetes, a logistic regression model based on eight factors predicted 3-year progression to diabetes with an AUC of 0.787."
