  "other_facts": "Objective: examine safety and efficacy of deferiprone in reducing motor disability progression in dopaminergic-treated and treatment-naïve patients with early-stage Parkinson's disease. SKY patients were randomized 1:1 to one of four dosage cohorts or placebo-matching cohorts (300, 600, 900, 1200 mg twice daily) for 9 months. EMBARK patients received deferiprone 15 mg/kg twice daily. Primary outcome for both studies was change from baseline to month 9 in MDS-UPDRS Part III motor examination score. ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT02728843; ANZCTR identifier: ACTRN12617001578392. Reducing nigrostriatal iron overload reduces neuronal loss in Parkinson's disease models. Parkinson's disease is an age-related brain condition that can lead to problems with movement, muscle cramps, mental health, and pain, and can reduce activities of daily living and well-being. Some people with Parkinson's disease have high levels of iron in their brain. It is unknown whether removing brain iron helps Parkinson's disease. The study included 176 people with Parkinson's disease divided into two groups. In the first group, people were already being treated for Parkinson's disease and were given deferiprone for 9 months; low or high dose deferiprone did not improve disease symptoms. In the second group, people taking no other medication for their Parkinson's disease were given deferiprone for 9 months; deferiprone worsened Parkinson's symptoms. Deferiprone combined with L-dopa does not provide significant motor function benefit, while deferiprone without L-dopa worsens symptoms. There are currently no arguments in favor of using deferiprone in Parkinson's disease. Research could evaluate low doses of iron chelator in combination with L-dopa over the long term and in large numbers of people.",
