  "other_facts": "Background: inflammation has recently become a focus of research in the development of gestational diabetes mellitus; SII and SIRI are novel indices reflecting the body's chronic immune-inflammatory state. Study aim: to investigate associations between SII or SIRI and gestational diabetes mellitus. Setting and period: Beijing Obstetrics and Gynecology Hospital, February 2018 to December 2020. Participants were recruited in their first trimester of pregnancy. Baseline SII and SIRI values were derived from routine clinical blood results. SII = neutrophil count × platelet count / lymphocyte count; SIRI = neutrophil count × monocyte count / lymphocyte count. Participants were grouped by quartiles of SII or SIRI. Gestational diabetes mellitus was assessed with a 75-g, 2-h oral glucose tolerance test at 24-28 weeks of gestation using International Association of Diabetes and Pregnancy Study Groups glucose thresholds. Logistic regression was used to analyze odds ratios with 95% confidence intervals for associations between SII, SIRI, and gestational diabetes mellitus risk. Higher SII and SIRI quartiles were correlated with increased gestational diabetes mellitus rates. SIRI findings paralleled SII outcomes. Associations were maintained in subgroup and sensitivity analyses. Conclusion: SII and SIRI are potential independent risk factors contributing to the onset of gestational diabetes mellitus.",
