  "other_facts": "Hidradenitis suppurativa is a chronic disease that causes painful skin lumps and scarring and starts in adolescence in an important number of patients. Adalimumab was the first biologic drug approved in adolescents with hidradenitis suppurativa, and approval was based on model-based extrapolation from other diseases; data regarding effectiveness and safety in this age group were lacking. The objective was to describe the effectiveness and safety of adalimumab in adolescents with moderate-to-severe hidradenitis suppurativa. HS units across Spain were invited to participate, including all patients starting adalimumab in adolescence; 9 HS units participated. Demographic, clinical, and treatment-related characteristics were retrieved. Effectiveness was measured as the proportion of patients achieving HiSCR at 6 months. Safety was assessed by quantification and description of side effects over time. The 3 patients requiring drug discontinuation had full recovery. Limitations included lack of a comparison group and the retrospective design. The authors concluded that adalimumab showed a similar safety profile in adolescents with moderate-to-severe hidradenitis suppurativa as in adults, with better effectiveness data.",
  "summary": "In a retrospective multicenter case series of 65 adolescents with moderate-to-severe hidradenitis suppurativa, adalimumab achieved HiSCR in 76.9% at 6 months and had side effects in 7 patients, including 3 drug discontinuations with full recovery."
