  "other_facts": "Obesity-related metabolic alterations are related to adipose tissue distribution; visceral fat predisposes to higher risk than subcutaneous fat. The effect of different forms of periodization of combined training on cardiometabolic risk in adults with obesity was previously unknown. Participants were allocated into three groups: control group (CG), combined training with fixed intensity (FG), and combined training with linear increase intensity (LG), in a 1:1:1 ratio. VAI equations used waist circumference, triglycerides, BMI, and HDL-c. Metabolic phenotype was defined by the presence of >3 abnormalities among systolic blood pressure, diastolic blood pressure, triglycerides, HDL-c, fasting blood glucose, and waist circumference. Intra- and intergroup analyses were performed per protocol and intention-to-treat using the Generalized Estimated Equations method. The significance level for interaction was p < 0.10, and for isolated effects of time and/or group was p < 0.05. Combined training with fixed intensity improved VAI but was insufficient to affect metabolic phenotype. The findings suggest minimal differences between fixed-intensity and linear-increase protocols in reducing the risk of metabolic complications during obesity treatment.",
