  "other_facts": "TAVR is increasingly utilized for severe aortic stenosis, yet optimal risk stratification remains challenging, particularly in patients with preserved ejection fraction. Diastolic dysfunction has been reported in up to 54.4% of TAVR patients (predominantly grade 1) and severe grade 3 diastolic dysfunction in ~13.4% in prior cohorts, and it predicts poor outcomes. NT-pro-BNP is a marker of ventricular stress. The study was conducted at Cleveland Clinic from 2016 to 2020. Of 1594 patients, 784 had complete echocardiographic diastolic dysfunction data. Pre- and post-TAVR NT-pro-BNP levels, clinical parameters, and echocardiographic parameters were analyzed. Diastolic dysfunction was classified using mitral valve E/e', tricuspid regurgitation velocity, and left atrial volume index. The Youden Index determined the optimal NT-pro-BNP cut-off, and outcomes were assessed via Kaplan-Meier and Cox regression analyses. Higher NT-pro-BNP tertiles were associated with worse baseline characteristics and echocardiographic diastolic dysfunction markers.",
