  "other_facts": "Nationally and in Hawaii, nurses are in crisis with high rates of distress, burnout, and intent to change jobs; organizations need evidence-based strategies to support nurse well-being. The study was informed by the National Academy of Medicine Factors Affecting Clinician Well-Being Model and aimed to identify individual and external factors associated with nurses' well-being. In December 2023, an online survey was conducted among hospital-based nurses from two major hospitals in Hawaii, representing various unit types. Survey measures included individual factors (role, personal characteristics, skills/abilities) and external factors (organizational, environmental) supporting clinician well-being. The well-being index ranged from -2 (excellent) to 9 (very poor). Analysis used general linear modeling with stepwise backward selection. No significant racial/ethnic difference in well-being was found. Factors significantly associated with worse well-being, indicated by increased average well-being scores, were working >=36 h/week, reports of experienced burnout, primary caregiving responsibility outside of work, lower self-reported physical health, and experience of workplace violence. Factors significantly associated with improved well-being, indicated by decreased average well-being scores, were higher personal resilience, no leadership responsibilities, belief that the organization is responsive to complaints and concerns, and appropriate ancillary staff to support work. Healthcare organizational leaders and policymakers must urgently correct system issues contributing to burnout, suboptimal mental health, decreased well-being, and attrition among nurses; healthcare organizations should cultivate wellness cultures and provide infrastructure offering evidence-based interventions to support nurses' well-being.",
