  "other_facts": "A footbath may be an accessible form of passive heat therapy to improve cardiovascular health. As a localized passive heat therapy stimulus, it was unclear whether longer durations are superior for promoting adaptive hemodynamic and vascular adjustments. Participants performed 30-, 45-, and 60-minute lower-leg hot-water immersions at 42°C and a 60-minute thermoneutral sham immersion at 36°C on 4 separate days. Superficial femoral artery blood flow and flow-mediated dilation were assessed pre- and postimmersion, and the time course of recovery was characterized over the 60 minutes following immersion. Twenty-four-hour ambulatory blood pressure was monitored over each experimental day. A 45-minute footbath resulted in comparable hemodynamic adjustments as 60 minutes of immersion and led to the largest acute improvement in vascular function. A 45-minute footbath may be the preferred dose of passive heat therapy for future interventions seeking to improve vascular health and blood pressure. A longer-duration footbath may not be better for cardiovascular benefits in older adults. Leg blood flow increases and ambulatory blood pressure reductions were similar with 30, 45, and 60 minutes of lower-leg hot-water immersion. Only a 45-minute footbath improved endothelial function compared with thermoneutral sham immersion. Forty-five minutes was identified as the most favorable acute dose of lower-leg hot-water immersion and may be most suitable for chronic interventions seeking to improve cardiovascular health.",
