  "other_facts": "Data were from the Medical Information Mart for Intensive Care-IV version 3.0 database. SHR was calculated based on initial blood glucose and glycated hemoglobin A1c levels. The primary end point was 7-day sepsis incidence diagnosed following Sepsis-3 criteria. P for the fully adjusted per-unit SHR hazard ratio was .04. Restricted cubic splines analysis showed a nonlinear saturation effect association, P for nonlinearity = .02; this was consistent in the diabetic subgroup, P for nonlinearity = .01. After adjusting for 7-day mortality as a competing event using the Fine-Gray model, SHR was independently associated with increased sepsis risk, P = .01. The SHR-sepsis association was significantly modified by diabetes mellitus, BMI, and insulin use, all P for interaction < .05. Mediation analysis showed that the systemic immune-inflammation index, neutrophil-to-lymphocyte ratio, platelet-to-neutrophil ratio, systemic inflammation response index, and monocyte-neutrophil-to-lymphocyte ratio significantly mediated the association in critically ill patients with heart failure. Systemic inflammatory indices partially mediated the association between elevated SHR and heightened 7-day sepsis risk, especially in patients with diabetes.",
