  "other_facts": "Kidney transplant recipients face a high risk of acute rejection, in which the immune system attacks the transplanted organ. Current diagnostics rely on invasive biopsies with procedural risks, costs, and limited temporal resolution. Urinary chemokines CXCL9 and CXCL10 are promising non-invasive acute rejection biomarkers, but clinical adoption is limited by labor-intensive detection and lack of point-of-care solutions. The biosensor uses 5 µL of unprocessed urine. ELISA and biopsy turnaround is 24-72 hours. The system uses screen-printed carbon electrodes modified with Ti3C2Tx MXene-crosslinked bovine serum albumin hydrogel, offering high conductivity, nano-porosity, anti-fouling properties, and signal stability for up to 30 days. The platform provides single-digit pg/mL-level sensitivity meeting clinical thresholds. The approach integrates advanced materials, biosensor engineering, and machine learning to provide a scalable, cost-effective point-of-care solution for real-time, non-invasive acute rejection monitoring, intended to reduce biopsy dependence, enable earlier intervention, and improve long-term transplant outcomes.",
