  "other_facts": "Obesity is described as one of the 21st century's greatest public health challenges; evidence on how inequalities intersect to shape obesity burden is scarce, particularly since the coronavirus disease 2019 pandemic. Whole-population, individual-level, anonymised electronic health records with life-course data for the entire population of adults aged 18-99 years in England were accessed via the National Health Service England Secure Data Environment. Age- and sex-standardised incidence and prevalence rates of obesity were estimated from 2019 to 2025, and negative binomial regression was used to examine trends by age, sex, socioeconomic status, ethnicity, and geographical region. Geographical region was defined using middle layer super output areas, neighbourhood-level units comprising 5,000 to 15,000 individuals. There was marked variation across subgroups in incidence and considerable variation in obesity prevalence by age, sex, socioeconomic status, and ethnicity. The prevalence in the most deprived Black women aged 60-69 years was nearly double that in the least-deprived White women in the same age group. Geographical disparities were nearly 6-fold, with the largest increases over time observed in the most deprived regions. Obesity affects nearly one in three adults in England. Increasing rates among individuals of childbearing age risk perpetuating intergenerational cycles of health inequality. The intersection of sociodemographic determinants underscores the preventable nature of obesity and the need to address dimensions of inequality in prevention efforts. Funding: Wellcome Trust, Health Data Research UK, British Heart Foundation Data Science Centre.",
