  "other_facts": "Background: global rise in nonagenarian and centenarian populations is increasing demand for surgical and critical care; acute biliary disease, particularly cholecystitis, often necessitates surgery; outcomes following cholecystectomy in oldest-old ICU patients remain poorly characterized. Objective: the study aimed to identify clinical and biochemical predictors of mortality and resource use in this cohort. Methods: the study used the Australian and New Zealand Intensive Care Society Adult Patient Database (2010-2024); patients aged >=90 years admitted to ICU after cholecystectomy for gallbladder-related disease were identified; primary endpoint was 1-year all-cause mortality after ICU admission; key secondary endpoints were ICU, in-hospital, 30-day, and 6-month mortality; exploratory endpoint was mortality at final follow-up censored 30 April 2025; secondary non-mortality outcomes were ICU and hospital length of stay; predefined admission predictors were APACHE III, SOFA, Glasgow Coma Scale, Clinical Frailty Scale, and peak 24-hour biochemical markers (urea, creatinine, albumin, bilirubin, white cell count); discrimination was quantified using the area under the receiver-operating-characteristic curve; associations with 1-year mortality were examined using multivariable logistic regression. Results: compared with 1-year survivors, non-survivors had higher APACHE III, SOFA, and frailty scores and more deranged admission biochemistry; APACHE III showed fair discrimination for 1-year mortality and outperformed SOFA, frailty, and Glasgow Coma Scale; serum urea provided the strongest discrimination among biochemical markers; in adjusted models, higher urea and bilirubin remained independently associated with 1-year mortality; high-risk groups defined by ROC-derived thresholds for APACHE III and urea had substantially higher 1-year mortality than low-risk groups. Conclusion: routine admission data, particularly APACHE III and simple biochemical markers