  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Appropriate antibiotic therapy remains a challenge in intensive care units. Guidelines recommend against using procalcitonin to guide the initiation of antibiotic therapy, although with low certainty and a very low level of evidence. Recent studies suggest that changes in procalcitonin levels may be more accurate than a single measurement; however, this has never been tested in a randomised trial. The trial aims to compare the efficacy and safety of two different procalcitonin protocols for initiating antibiotic therapy. Randomisation is in a 1:1 ratio. In the Kinetics Group, initiation of antibiotic treatment is strongly recommended if PCT >=0.5 ng/mL and increases by more than 100% compared with the previous day. In the Absolute Group, initiation of antibiotic treatment is strongly recommended if PCT >=0.5 ng/mL. The primary outcome will be assessed 72 hours after recruitment by an independent intensivist, microbiologist and infectologist using all relevant clinical, microbiological and radiographic results; if no consensus is reached, a decision will be made by secret vote. Planned enrolment is 250 participants, with 80% statistical power and a 5% alpha level. Ethics approval was obtained from the National Centre for Public Health and Pharmacy (BM/20019/2025). Results are planned for presentation at national and international conferences, publication in a peer-reviewed journal, and dissemination to important stakeholders in the Hungarian healthcare system. This is the first version of the trial protocol, prepared in accordance with the SPIRIT 2025 guideline (version 1.0, dated 30 September 2025). The trial has been registered on ClinicalTrials.gov (NCT07211620).",
  "summary": "This multicentre randomized controlled trial plans to enroll 250 critically ill adults with suspected new-onset infection to compare procalcitonin kinetics-guided versus absolute-threshold protocols for initiating antibiotic therapy, with the primary outcome of unnecessary antibiotic therapy assessed 72 hours after recruitment."
}

