  "other_facts": "Setting: Organ Procurement and Transplantation Network registry, January 1, 2018 to December 31, 2023. Children in the United States receive priority for kidneys with KDPI <35%, although their priority falls below that of certain adult recipients. The KDPI was revised from 10 donor characteristics to 8 donor characteristics by excluding donor race and donor hepatitis C virus infection. Objectives were to describe allocation of high-quality kidneys and compare donor characteristics using KDPI-10 versus KDPI-8. Primary outcome was use of kidneys with KDPI <35% overall; secondary outcome was utilization of kidneys from ideal pediatric-quality donors; additional assessment was changes in characteristics of KDPI <35% donors after KDPI revision. Analytical approach: descriptive statistics. Limitation: extrapolation of KDPI-8 donor characteristics using a historical cohort. Conclusion: many ideal pediatric-quality kidneys are allocated to adults, primarily multiorgan transplant recipients, and no meaningful change is anticipated in the proportion of ideal pediatric-quality kidneys prioritized for children using KDPI-8. Plain-language summary facts: children with end-stage kidney disease are intended to have priority for kidneys from best-quality donors; priority may fall below adult candidates needing multiple organs or highly sensitized candidates; only about one-third of donors prioritized for children are ideal pediatric quality, meaning they come from young donors with minimal risk of kidney dysfunction; about one-third of these top-quality kidneys are transplanted into adults with more priority than children, most often recipients receiving more than one organ transplant; the revised risk index excludes donor race and donor hepatitis C infection; the change is suggested not to substantially affect the proportion of ideal pediatric-quality kidneys offered to children.",
